{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:45:12.115Z","ordinal":9616,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_068dffc2438c6825016a8cbb66b76087d082c0387f17cdd7db","status":"completed","call_id":"call_7JFHCJiRsn07E3rwvglEQn6W","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rg -n -C 4 \\\"pre-push|trust|Gemini|Claude|global|Remove|removed|settings-only|one-line hold|no-op|hook\\\" docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/Documents - Peyton’s MacBook Pro/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/global-settings-tier\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","create_time":1787607902.740111}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:45:12.186Z","ordinal":9617,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a025ce-e754-7631-8467-eaa6035ba0f8","turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-714c5e6a-61fd-44dd-9e20-c70677ee66fb","process_id":"52657","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -C 4 \"pre-push|trust|Gemini|Claude|global|Remove|removed|settings-only|one-line hold|no-op|hook\" docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/peytonmontei/Documents/Documents%20-%20Peyton%E2%80%99s%20MacBook%20Pro/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/global-settings-tier","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n -C 4 'pre-push|trust|Gemini|Claude|global|Remove|removed|settings-only|one-line hold|no-op|hook' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","query":"pre-push|trust|Gemini|Claude|global|Remove|removed|settings-only|one-line hold|no-op|hook","path":"2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"1-# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n2-\n3-**Status:** Approved for implementation planning  \n4-**Date:** 2026-08-24  \n5:**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030  \n6:**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n7-\n8-## Context\n9-\n10:The consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n11-\n12:1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n13-2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n14:3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n15:4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n16:5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n17-\n18-The combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\n19-the APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\n20-activation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\n21:agent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\n22-must participate in.\n23-\n24-The desired product contract is now narrower:\n25-\n26:- all global tracking configuration is edited only in the user's\n27-  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n28-  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n29:- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that mutate the `global`\n30-  settings block;\n31:- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n32-  inert until repository policy activates them; and\n33-- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n34-\n35-Trail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\n--\n37-transitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n38-\n39-## Goals\n40-\n41:- Make the user settings file the sole write surface for global activation,\n42:  exclusions, and trust.\n43:- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n44-  policy.\n45:- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n46-  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n47-- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n48-  process through one typed policy result.\n49-- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n50:  or trusted the repository.\n51-- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n52:  global policy.\n53-- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n54-- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n55-  isolation.\n56-- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n57-  affected behavior.\n58-\n59-## Non-goals\n60-\n61:- A CLI editor for global settings.\n62:- A global-enablement or trust wizard.\n63:- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n64-  and deduplication contract.\n65-- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n66-  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n67-- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n--\n72-## User contract\n73-\n74-### Global settings are file-only\n75-\n76:The `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n77-\n78-```json\n79-{\n80:  \"global\": {\n81-    \"enabled\": true,\n82-    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n83-    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n84-    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n85:    \"trust_all\": false,\n86:    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n87:    \"trusted_paths\": []\n88-  }\n89-}\n90-```\n91-\n92-The refactor removes every CLI mutation path for this block:\n93-\n94:- `entire enable --global`;\n95:- `entire disable --global`;\n96:- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question;\n97:- `entire trust` and `entire trust --revoke`; and\n98:- the interactive pre-push trust prompt, including the \"Always\" writer.\n99-\n100-Status, doctor, and hold messages may show the resolved settings path and the\n101:specific keys a user needs to edit. They must not mutate the global block.\n102-\n103:### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n104-\n105:Entire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n106:(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\n107-and the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\n108:missing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n109-\n110:The installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\n111:policy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\n112-tracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n113-\n114:Hook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\n115-a supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\n116-integration-changing command or doctor repair.\n117-\n118-## Repository policy\n--\n138-\n139-Exact names may follow local conventions. `ClassifyRepoPolicy` never writes and\n140-may return a proposed layout when no route exists. Status, doctor, help, auth,\n141-and other read-only commands use this result directly and create no marker,\n142:directory, hook, or settings file.\n143-\n144-Runtime-writing callers use a separate `EnsureRuntimeRoute` operation. It\n145-atomically establishes the proposed route, rereads the winner of any race, and\n146-returns a new immutable policy snapshot containing the selected route. That\n147:snapshot is passed through context to hook dispatch, lazy setup, logging, and\n148-egress paths. Consumers must not reconstruct policy from overlapping boolean\n149-helpers or continue using the pre-establishment snapshot.\n150-\n151-This leaf package owns:\n152-\n153:- user-global settings loading needed by the gate;\n154-- repository activation-marker loading;\n155-- strict repository-settings provenance checks;\n156-- origin and path identity normalization;\n157:- exclusion and trust matching; and\n158-- runtime-route marker resolution.\n159-\n160-The `settings` package remains the public typed configuration API. The `paths`\n161-package constructs paths from a resolved layout and does not read or decode\n--\n164-### Activation provenance\n165-\n166-Repository content is configuration, not local activation evidence.\n167-`<worktree>/.entire/settings.json` containing `\"enabled\": true` does not by\n168:itself activate user-level hooks and never grants egress consent.\n169-\n170-Ordinary `entire enable` records a machine-local activation marker beneath the\n171-current worktree's namespace in the Git common directory. The marker is not\n172-cloned, committed, or shared between linked worktrees.\n173-\n174:Legacy bootstrap is deliberately narrower than “an Entire Git hook exists.” A\n175-single-worktree repository may bootstrap from an enabled repository setting plus\n176:an Entire hook installed inside its actual Git directory, because that hook\n177:cannot arrive in a clone. Linked worktrees share the common hooks directory, so\n178:common-hook presence is never worktree-specific evidence there. A linked\n179-worktree bootstraps only from verified untracked, non-symlinked runtime evidence\n180-inside that exact worktree or from an explicit `entire enable` run in that\n181:worktree. A tracked `core.hooksPath` inside the worktree is never activation\n182-evidence.\n183-\n184-Accepted worktree-specific legacy evidence is intentionally exact:\n185-\n--\n189-  `.entire/metadata/<session-id>/` (`prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `full.log`,\n190-  `transcript.jsonl`, `metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`, or\n191-  `content_hash.txt`).\n192-\n193:Empty directories, logs, tmp files, project settings, shared/common hooks, and\n194:any external or worktree-relative `core.hooksPath` are not bootstrap evidence.\n195-\n196-A repository-level `enabled: false` may remain an explicit capture veto. An\n197:`enabled: true` value configures an already locally or globally activated\n198-repository but does not create activation.\n199-\n200-### Settings provenance and executable values\n201-\n202-Hook-time settings are exposed through a sanitized view selected by activation\n203-source. Foreground commands unrelated to capture retain their current settings\n204:behavior; this matrix governs agent hooks, Git hooks, checkpoint creation, and\n205-checkpoint egress.\n206-\n207-| Settings field/group | Global-only activation | Local activation |\n208-| --- | --- | --- |\n--\n211-| Built-in declarative redaction (`pii`, `custom_redactions`, `externalize_images`) | Allowed; malformed values fail closed | Existing merged behavior |\n212-| External scanner/OPF configuration (`openai_privacy_filter`, `betterleaks`, `goredact`) | Ignored; compiled safe defaults only | Existing behavior, including the rule that `opf_command` must come from verified untracked `settings.local.json` |\n213-| `strategy_options`, including `checkpoint_remote` | Ignored; default checkpoint strategy and destination derivation | Existing behavior and inherited-remote ownership checks |\n214-| `checkpoints`, `sign_checkpoint_commits`, `vercel` | Ignored | Existing behavior |\n215:| `absolute_git_hook_path`, `external_agents` | Ignored; global infrastructure uses built-in supported agents only | Existing behavior |\n216-| `commit_linking` | Ignored; compiled default | Existing behavior |\n217:| Telemetry, review, investigate, and summary-generation settings | Not consumed by the hook-time sanitized view | Unchanged for their existing foreground consumers |\n218-\n219-The whitelist is implemented positively; adding a new `EntireSettings` field\n220:does not automatically make it available to global-only hooks. A test must\n221-classify every new field before the sanitized-view switch can expose it.\n222-\n223-Local settings paths are rejected as locally owned if the file or any parent\n224-path component traverses a symlink. Tracked, unreadable, malformed, or\n225:unverifiable local configuration fails toward the untrusted classification.\n226-Local activation does not relax existing per-field provenance checks: in\n227-particular, a project-file `opf_command` remains rejected after `entire enable`.\n228-Symlink validation starts from the canonical worktree root and inspects only the\n229-worktree-relative `.entire/settings*.json` components with `lstat`; it does not\n--\n244-\n245-- an existing explicitly enabled repository with local `.entire` runtime data\n246-  keeps the worktree layout;\n247-- a new `entire enable` with no prior route selects the worktree layout when\n248:  global policy is inactive, or the git-common layout when global policy is\n249-  already active;\n250:- a repository first activated by user-global policy selects the hidden\n251-  git-common-dir layout; and\n252:- later transitions between global and local activation do not move runtime\n253-  data or change the selected route.\n254-\n255-Route and activation records always live in a per-worktree registry beneath the\n256-clone's Git common directory:\n--\n288-\n289-### Atomic route selection\n290-\n291-The first process that needs runtime storage establishes the route before writing\n292:runtime data. Read-only commands never call this operation. Competing global-hook\n293-and `entire enable` processes use a short, bounded route-selection critical\n294-section:\n295-\n296-1. derive the deterministic per-worktree marker path;\n297-2. acquire the per-worktree route-selection lock only for read/create/publish;\n298-3. write and fsync a temporary complete marker, then atomically publish it\n299-   without replacing an existing marker;\n300-4. on collision, read and use the winner; and\n301:5. on an unverifiable result, skip hook capture rather than guessing a route.\n302-\n303:The proposal is deterministic for the normal race: while global policy is\n304:active, both a first global hook and concurrent `entire enable` propose the\n305:git-common layout. With global policy inactive, explicit enable proposes the\n306-worktree layout. A conflicting proposal caused by a concurrent settings edit is\n307-resolved by first published route wins.\n308-\n309-No long-held migration lock is needed. Hooks do not block behind interactive\n310-input or condensation, and lock failure cannot fail a Git commit, push, or agent\n311-session.\n312-\n313-The existing takeover planner, recursive mover, transcript-path repair,\n314:migration gate, retry marker, and test-only migration wrappers are removed.\n315-\n316-### Activation and route transitions\n317-\n318-| Starting state and action | Result |\n319-| --- | --- |\n320:| No markers, global off | Inactive; read-only commands make no writes |\n321:| No markers, global on; first hook | Select git-common route, leave local activation absent, capture globally |\n322:| No markers, global off; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select worktree route, then atomically set local activation `enabled` |\n323:| No markers, global on; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select git-common route, then set local activation `enabled` |\n324-| Existing route; `entire enable` | Preserve route and set local activation `enabled` |\n325:| `entire disable` | Set local activation `disabled`, preserve route and runtime data; this is a per-worktree veto over global activation. A global-only repo writes no worktree file; a locally activated repo also preserves the existing repo-settings disable behavior |\n326-| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n327:| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n328-| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n329:| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n330-| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n331-| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n332-| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n333-\n334-If repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n335:`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\n336:inactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\n337-policy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n338-\n339:## Egress trust\n340-\n341-### File-defined consent\n342-\n343:For a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\n344:active global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n345-\n346:- `global.trust_all` is true;\n347-- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n348:  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n349:- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n350-\n351-All errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\n352:normalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\n353:URL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\n354:fallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\n355:inactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\n356-when capture is inactive.\n357-\n358:Because the CLI no longer mutates trust entries, there is no asymmetric identity\n359-pruning or revoke operation. A stale origin entry remains explicit user\n360-configuration; removing that entry from the settings file is the revocation.\n361:Status reports the exact currently matched trust source.\n362-\n363-Locally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\n364-behavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\n365-inferred from committed settings.\n366-\n367-### Push behavior\n368-\n369:Pre-push never prompts. If an untrusted globally activated repository has no\n370:pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing. If checkpoints are pending, the\n371:hook prints one single-line hold notice, returns success, and leaves the user's\n372-normal branch push untouched. The notice points to the resolved user settings\n373-file instead of an unavailable command.\n374-\n375-Every checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\n376:Doctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\n377-gate merely because it is interactive.\n378-\n379:## Agent hook model\n380-\n381:Each supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n382-\n383-- installation;\n384-- recognition and version checks;\n385-- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n386-- removal of stale Entire entries;\n387-- expected counts; and\n388-- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n389-\n390:Gemini hooks whose parser always returns no lifecycle event are not installed:\n391-`AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`, and\n392-`Notification`.\n393:Legacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\n394-reconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\n395-commands.\n396-\n397:An agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\n398-lazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\n399-settings or re-running Git probes.\n400-\n401-### Hook reconciliation operations\n--\n408-- `entire enable`;\n409-- `entire agent add`; and\n410-- doctor's confirmed repair path.\n411-\n412:It does not run from hooks or read-only commands such as `status`, `help`,\n413-`version`, `auth token`, or JSON inspection commands. Reconciliation targets\n414-only supported agents positively detected as installed, or agents whose user\n415-configuration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\n416-only. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\n417-repo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n418-\n419-Each agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\n420-read-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\n421:enable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\n422:doctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\n423-process or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n424-\n425-Lazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\n426:current Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n427:`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\n428-doctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\n429:make a later hook repair it.\n430-\n431-## Status, doctor, and errors\n432-\n433-Status reports:\n434-\n435:- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n436-- the resolved settings-file path;\n437-- activation source for the current repository;\n438-- runtime layout;\n439:- trust source or held state; and\n440:- user-hook installation health.\n441-\n442:Doctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\n443:the global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\n444:settings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n445-\n446:Policy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\n447-migration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\n448-user's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\n449-aborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\n450-behavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\n451:other established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\n452-to fail that push.\n453-\n454-## Findings disposition\n455-\n456-| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n457-| --- | --- | --- |\n458:| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove migration gate and interactive trust prompt | Pending and non-pending pre-push cases never read stdin; non-pending is silent; pending emits one line; concurrent hook and push do not block |\n459:| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n460:| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n461:| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n462:| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Remove pre-push trust prompt | `ACCESSIBLE=1` pre-push remains non-interactive, held, and panic-free |\n463:| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n464:| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Remove trust writers/revoke; use pure current-identity matching over explicit settings | Origin→path→origin and multiple fetch/push URL cases authorize only when every current key is explicitly trusted |\n465:| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n466-\n467:Two older GitHub comments are also covered: the removed prompt can no longer\n468-misstate folder-versus-origin scope, and the remaining hold message is exactly\n469-one line.\n470-\n471-## Testing strategy\n--\n473-### Policy matrix\n474-\n475-Table-driven tests cover combinations of:\n476-\n477:- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n478-- local activation marker absent or present;\n479-- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n480-- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n481-- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n482:- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n483-\n484-Every case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\n485:decision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n486-\n487-### Runtime and concurrency\n488-\n489-- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n490:- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n491:- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n492-- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n493-  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n494-  recorded independently.\n495-- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n496-- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n497-  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n498-- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n499:  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n500:- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n501:  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n502-  in both cases.\n503-- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n504-- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n505-  because those components no longer exist.\n506-\n507-### Hooks and security\n508-\n509:- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n510:- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n511:- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n512:- Gemini no-op lifecycle hooks are absent.\n513-- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n514-  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n515-- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n516-  malicious committed-value test.\n517:- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n518:  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n519:- Accessible mode cannot grant consent or consume pre-push stdin because the\n520-  prompt is absent.\n521-- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n522:  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n523-\n524-### Integration\n525-\n526-Real-binary integration tests cover:\n527-\n528:1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n529:2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> successful branch push plus one-line hold;\n530:3. settings-file origin/path trust -> queued checkpoint drain;\n531:4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n532-   session visibility;\n533-5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n534-   behavior; and\n535-6. linked-worktree isolation.\n--\n545-commits:\n546-\n547-1. policy and provenance model;\n548-2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n549:3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n550:4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n551-5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n552-6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n553-\n554-PR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\n--\n564-\n565-### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n566-\n567-This is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n568:long-lived hook locks, route-transition races, and most of the current test\n569-surface. It does not address the architectural cause of the findings.\n570-\n571-### Move every repository to git-side runtime storage\n572-\n573-A universal git-common-dir runtime is the cleanest abstract model, but it would\n574-force a migration on every existing explicitly enabled repository and change\n575-established `.entire/logs`, `metadata`, and `tmp` behavior. That expansion is not\n576:required to make the unmerged global feature coherent.\n577-\n578:### Keep interactive trust while making activation settings-only\n579-\n580-This retains a convenient consent prompt but violates the requirement that the\n581:global block is writable only through the settings file. It also preserves the\n582:accessible-mode stdin hazard and keeps human input inside pre-push execution.\n583-\n584-### Infer local activation from committed project settings\n585-\n586-This preserves the current short circuit but lets cloned content grant egress\n587-and unlock executable configuration. Machine-local activation evidence is the\n588:necessary security boundary once user-level hooks are installed everywhere.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"1-# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n2-\n3-**Status:** Approved for implementation planning  \n4-**Date:** 2026-08-24  \n5:**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030  \n6:**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n7-\n8-## Context\n9-\n10:The consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n11-\n12:1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n13-2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n14:3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n15:4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n16:5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n17-\n18-The combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\n19-the APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\n20-activation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\n21:agent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\n22-must participate in.\n23-\n24-The desired product contract is now narrower:\n25-\n26:- all global tracking configuration is edited only in the user's\n27-  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n28-  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n29:- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that mutate the `global`\n30-  settings block;\n31:- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n32-  inert until repository policy activates them; and\n33-- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n34-\n35-Trail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\n--\n37-transitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n38-\n39-## Goals\n40-\n41:- Make the user settings file the sole write surface for global activation,\n42:  exclusions, and trust.\n43:- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n44-  policy.\n45:- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n46-  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n47-- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n48-  process through one typed policy result.\n49-- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n50:  or trusted the repository.\n51-- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n52:  global policy.\n53-- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n54-- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n55-  isolation.\n56-- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n57-  affected behavior.\n58-\n59-## Non-goals\n60-\n61:- A CLI editor for global settings.\n62:- A global-enablement or trust wizard.\n63:- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n64-  and deduplication contract.\n65-- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n66-  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n67-- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n--\n72-## User contract\n73-\n74-### Global settings are file-only\n75-\n76:The `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n77-\n78-```json\n79-{\n80:  \"global\": {\n81-    \"enabled\": true,\n82-    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n83-    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n84-    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n85:    \"trust_all\": false,\n86:    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n87:    \"trusted_paths\": []\n88-  }\n89-}\n90-```\n91-\n92-The refactor removes every CLI mutation path for this block:\n93-\n94:- `entire enable --global`;\n95:- `entire disable --global`;\n96:- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question;\n97:- `entire trust` and `entire trust --revoke`; and\n98:- the interactive pre-push trust prompt, including the \"Always\" writer.\n99-\n100-Status, doctor, and hold messages may show the resolved settings path and the\n101:specific keys a user needs to edit. They must not mutate the global block.\n102-\n103:### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n104-\n105:Entire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n106:(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\n107-and the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\n108:missing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n109-\n110:The installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\n111:policy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\n112-tracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n113-\n114:Hook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\n115-a supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\n116-integration-changing command or doctor repair.\n117-\n118-## Repository policy\n--\n138-\n139-Exact names may follow local conventions. `ClassifyRepoPolicy` never writes and\n140-may return a proposed layout when no route exists. Status, doctor, help, auth,\n141-and other read-only commands use this result directly and create no marker,\n142:directory, hook, or settings file.\n143-\n144-Runtime-writing callers use a separate `EnsureRuntimeRoute` operation. It\n145-atomically establishes the proposed route, rereads the winner of any race, and\n146-returns a new immutable policy snapshot containing the selected route. That\n147:snapshot is passed through context to hook dispatch, lazy setup, logging, and\n148-egress paths. Consumers must not reconstruct policy from overlapping boolean\n149-helpers or continue using the pre-establishment snapshot.\n150-\n151-This leaf package owns:\n152-\n153:- user-global settings loading needed by the gate;\n154-- repository activation-marker loading;\n155-- strict repository-settings provenance checks;\n156-- origin and path identity normalization;\n157:- exclusion and trust matching; and\n158-- runtime-route marker resolution.\n159-\n160-The `settings` package remains the public typed configuration API. The `paths`\n161-package constructs paths from a resolved layout and does not read or decode\n--\n164-### Activation provenance\n165-\n166-Repository content is configuration, not local activation evidence.\n167-`<worktree>/.entire/settings.json` containing `\"enabled\": true` does not by\n168:itself activate user-level hooks and never grants egress consent.\n169-\n170-Ordinary `entire enable` records a machine-local activation marker beneath the\n171-current worktree's namespace in the Git common directory. The marker is not\n172-cloned, committed, or shared between linked worktrees.\n173-\n174:Legacy bootstrap is deliberately narrower than “an Entire Git hook exists.” A\n175-single-worktree repository may bootstrap from an enabled repository setting plus\n176:an Entire hook installed inside its actual Git directory, because that hook\n177:cannot arrive in a clone. Linked worktrees share the common hooks directory, so\n178:common-hook presence is never worktree-specific evidence there. A linked\n179-worktree bootstraps only from verified untracked, non-symlinked runtime evidence\n180-inside that exact worktree or from an explicit `entire enable` run in that\n181:worktree. A tracked `core.hooksPath` inside the worktree is never activation\n182-evidence.\n183-\n184-Accepted worktree-specific legacy evidence is intentionally exact:\n185-\n--\n189-  `.entire/metadata/<session-id>/` (`prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `full.log`,\n190-  `transcript.jsonl`, `metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`, or\n191-  `content_hash.txt`).\n192-\n193:Empty directories, logs, tmp files, project settings, shared/common hooks, and\n194:any external or worktree-relative `core.hooksPath` are not bootstrap evidence.\n195-\n196-A repository-level `enabled: false` may remain an explicit capture veto. An\n197:`enabled: true` value configures an already locally or globally activated\n198-repository but does not create activation.\n199-\n200-### Settings provenance and executable values\n201-\n202-Hook-time settings are exposed through a sanitized view selected by activation\n203-source. Foreground commands unrelated to capture retain their current settings\n204:behavior; this matrix governs agent hooks, Git hooks, checkpoint creation, and\n205-checkpoint egress.\n206-\n207-| Settings field/group | Global-only activation | Local activation |\n208-| --- | --- | --- |\n--\n211-| Built-in declarative redaction (`pii`, `custom_redactions`, `externalize_images`) | Allowed; malformed values fail closed | Existing merged behavior |\n212-| External scanner/OPF configuration (`openai_privacy_filter`, `betterleaks`, `goredact`) | Ignored; compiled safe defaults only | Existing behavior, including the rule that `opf_command` must come from verified untracked `settings.local.json` |\n213-| `strategy_options`, including `checkpoint_remote` | Ignored; default checkpoint strategy and destination derivation | Existing behavior and inherited-remote ownership checks |\n214-| `checkpoints`, `sign_checkpoint_commits`, `vercel` | Ignored | Existing behavior |\n215:| `absolute_git_hook_path`, `external_agents` | Ignored; global infrastructure uses built-in supported agents only | Existing behavior |\n216-| `commit_linking` | Ignored; compiled default | Existing behavior |\n217:| Telemetry, review, investigate, and summary-generation settings | Not consumed by the hook-time sanitized view | Unchanged for their existing foreground consumers |\n218-\n219-The whitelist is implemented positively; adding a new `EntireSettings` field\n220:does not automatically make it available to global-only hooks. A test must\n221-classify every new field before the sanitized-view switch can expose it.\n222-\n223-Local settings paths are rejected as locally owned if the file or any parent\n224-path component traverses a symlink. Tracked, unreadable, malformed, or\n225:unverifiable local configuration fails toward the untrusted classification.\n226-Local activation does not relax existing per-field provenance checks: in\n227-particular, a project-file `opf_command` remains rejected after `entire enable`.\n228-Symlink validation starts from the canonical worktree root and inspects only the\n229-worktree-relative `.entire/settings*.json` components with `lstat`; it does not\n--\n244-\n245-- an existing explicitly enabled repository with local `.entire` runtime data\n246-  keeps the worktree layout;\n247-- a new `entire enable` with no prior route selects the worktree layout when\n248:  global policy is inactive, or the git-common layout when global policy is\n249-  already active;\n250:- a repository first activated by user-global policy selects the hidden\n251-  git-common-dir layout; and\n252:- later transitions between global and local activation do not move runtime\n253-  data or change the selected route.\n254-\n255-Route and activation records always live in a per-worktree registry beneath the\n256-clone's Git common directory:\n--\n288-\n289-### Atomic route selection\n290-\n291-The first process that needs runtime storage establishes the route before writing\n292:runtime data. Read-only commands never call this operation. Competing global-hook\n293-and `entire enable` processes use a short, bounded route-selection critical\n294-section:\n295-\n296-1. derive the deterministic per-worktree marker path;\n297-2. acquire the per-worktree route-selection lock only for read/create/publish;\n298-3. write and fsync a temporary complete marker, then atomically publish it\n299-   without replacing an existing marker;\n300-4. on collision, read and use the winner; and\n301:5. on an unverifiable result, skip hook capture rather than guessing a route.\n302-\n303:The proposal is deterministic for the normal race: while global policy is\n304:active, both a first global hook and concurrent `entire enable` propose the\n305:git-common layout. With global policy inactive, explicit enable proposes the\n306-worktree layout. A conflicting proposal caused by a concurrent settings edit is\n307-resolved by first published route wins.\n308-\n309-No long-held migration lock is needed. Hooks do not block behind interactive\n310-input or condensation, and lock failure cannot fail a Git commit, push, or agent\n311-session.\n312-\n313-The existing takeover planner, recursive mover, transcript-path repair,\n314:migration gate, retry marker, and test-only migration wrappers are removed.\n315-\n316-### Activation and route transitions\n317-\n318-| Starting state and action | Result |\n319-| --- | --- |\n320:| No markers, global off | Inactive; read-only commands make no writes |\n321:| No markers, global on; first hook | Select git-common route, leave local activation absent, capture globally |\n322:| No markers, global off; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select worktree route, then atomically set local activation `enabled` |\n323:| No markers, global on; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select git-common route, then set local activation `enabled` |\n324-| Existing route; `entire enable` | Preserve route and set local activation `enabled` |\n325:| `entire disable` | Set local activation `disabled`, preserve route and runtime data; this is a per-worktree veto over global activation. A global-only repo writes no worktree file; a locally activated repo also preserves the existing repo-settings disable behavior |\n326-| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n327:| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n328-| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n329:| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n330-| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n331-| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n332-| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n333-\n334-If repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n335:`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\n336:inactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\n337-policy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n338-\n339:## Egress trust\n340-\n341-### File-defined consent\n342-\n343:For a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\n344:active global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n345-\n346:- `global.trust_all` is true;\n347-- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n348:  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n349:- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n350-\n351-All errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\n352:normalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\n353:URL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\n354:fallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\n355:inactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\n356-when capture is inactive.\n357-\n358:Because the CLI no longer mutates trust entries, there is no asymmetric identity\n359-pruning or revoke operation. A stale origin entry remains explicit user\n360-configuration; removing that entry from the settings file is the revocation.\n361:Status reports the exact currently matched trust source.\n362-\n363-Locally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\n364-behavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\n365-inferred from committed settings.\n366-\n367-### Push behavior\n368-\n369:Pre-push never prompts. If an untrusted globally activated repository has no\n370:pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing. If checkpoints are pending, the\n371:hook prints one single-line hold notice, returns success, and leaves the user's\n372-normal branch push untouched. The notice points to the resolved user settings\n373-file instead of an unavailable command.\n374-\n375-Every checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\n376:Doctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\n377-gate merely because it is interactive.\n378-\n379:## Agent hook model\n380-\n381:Each supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n382-\n383-- installation;\n384-- recognition and version checks;\n385-- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n386-- removal of stale Entire entries;\n387-- expected counts; and\n388-- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n389-\n390:Gemini hooks whose parser always returns no lifecycle event are not installed:\n391-`AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`, and\n392-`Notification`.\n393:Legacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\n394-reconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\n395-commands.\n396-\n397:An agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\n398-lazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\n399-settings or re-running Git probes.\n400-\n401-### Hook reconciliation operations\n--\n408-- `entire enable`;\n409-- `entire agent add`; and\n410-- doctor's confirmed repair path.\n411-\n412:It does not run from hooks or read-only commands such as `status`, `help`,\n413-`version`, `auth token`, or JSON inspection commands. Reconciliation targets\n414-only supported agents positively detected as installed, or agents whose user\n415-configuration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\n416-only. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\n417-repo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n418-\n419-Each agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\n420-read-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\n421:enable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\n422:doctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\n423-process or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n424-\n425-Lazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\n426:current Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n427:`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\n428-doctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\n429:make a later hook repair it.\n430-\n431-## Status, doctor, and errors\n432-\n433-Status reports:\n434-\n435:- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n436-- the resolved settings-file path;\n437-- activation source for the current repository;\n438-- runtime layout;\n439:- trust source or held state; and\n440:- user-hook installation health.\n441-\n442:Doctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\n443:the global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\n444:settings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n445-\n446:Policy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\n447-migration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\n448-user's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\n449-aborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\n450-behavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\n451:other established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\n452-to fail that push.\n453-\n454-## Findings disposition\n455-\n456-| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n457-| --- | --- | --- |\n458:| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove migration gate and interactive trust prompt | Pending and non-pending pre-push cases never read stdin; non-pending is silent; pending emits one line; concurrent hook and push do not block |\n459:| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n460:| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n461:| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n462:| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Remove pre-push trust prompt | `ACCESSIBLE=1` pre-push remains non-interactive, held, and panic-free |\n463:| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n464:| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Remove trust writers/revoke; use pure current-identity matching over explicit settings | Origin→path→origin and multiple fetch/push URL cases authorize only when every current key is explicitly trusted |\n465:| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n466-\n467:Two older GitHub comments are also covered: the removed prompt can no longer\n468-misstate folder-versus-origin scope, and the remaining hold message is exactly\n469-one line.\n470-\n471-## Testing strategy\n--\n473-### Policy matrix\n474-\n475-Table-driven tests cover combinations of:\n476-\n477:- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n478-- local activation marker absent or present;\n479-- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n480-- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n481-- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n482:- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n483-\n484-Every case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\n485:decision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n486-\n487-### Runtime and concurrency\n488-\n489-- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n490:- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n491:- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n492-- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n493-  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n494-  recorded independently.\n495-- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n496-- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n497-  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n498-- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n499:  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n500:- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n501:  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n502-  in both cases.\n503-- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n504-- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n505-  because those components no longer exist.\n506-\n507-### Hooks and security\n508-\n509:- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n510:- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n511:- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n512:- Gemini no-op lifecycle hooks are absent.\n513-- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n514-  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n515-- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n516-  malicious committed-value test.\n517:- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n518:  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n519:- Accessible mode cannot grant consent or consume pre-push stdin because the\n520-  prompt is absent.\n521-- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n522:  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n523-\n524-### Integration\n525-\n526-Real-binary integration tests cover:\n527-\n528:1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n529:2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> successful branch push plus one-line hold;\n530:3. settings-file origin/path trust -> queued checkpoint drain;\n531:4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n532-   session visibility;\n533-5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n534-   behavior; and\n535-6. linked-worktree isolation.\n--\n545-commits:\n546-\n547-1. policy and provenance model;\n548-2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n549:3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n550:4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n551-5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n552-6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n553-\n554-PR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\n--\n564-\n565-### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n566-\n567-This is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n568:long-lived hook locks, route-transition races, and most of the current test\n569-surface. It does not address the architectural cause of the findings.\n570-\n571-### Move every repository to git-side runtime storage\n572-\n573-A universal git-common-dir runtime is the cleanest abstract model, but it would\n574-force a migration on every existing explicitly enabled repository and change\n575-established `.entire/logs`, `metadata`, and `tmp` behavior. That expansion is not\n576:required to make the unmerged global feature coherent.\n577-\n578:### Keep interactive trust while making activation settings-only\n579-\n580-This retains a convenient consent prompt but violates the requirement that the\n581:global block is writable only through the settings file. It also preserves the\n582:accessible-mode stdin hazard and keeps human input inside pre-push execution.\n583-\n584-### Infer local activation from committed project settings\n585-\n586-This preserves the current short circuit but lets cloned content grant egress\n587-and unlock executable configuration. Machine-local activation evidence is the\n588:necessary security boundary once user-level hooks are installed everywhere.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":3125},"formatted_output":"1-# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n2-\n3-**Status:** Approved for implementation planning  \n4-**Date:** 2026-08-24  \n5:**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030  \n6:**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n7-\n8-## Context\n9-\n10:The consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n11-\n12:1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n13-2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n14:3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n15:4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n16:5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n17-\n18-The combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\n19-the APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\n20-activation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\n21:agent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\n22-must participate in.\n23-\n24-The desired product contract is now narrower:\n25-\n26:- all global tracking configuration is edited only in the user's\n27-  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n28-  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n29:- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that mutate the `global`\n30-  settings block;\n31:- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n32-  inert until repository policy activates them; and\n33-- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n34-\n35-Trail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\n--\n37-transitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n38-\n39-## Goals\n40-\n41:- Make the user settings file the sole write surface for global activation,\n42:  exclusions, and trust.\n43:- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n44-  policy.\n45:- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n46-  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n47-- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n48-  process through one typed policy result.\n49-- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n50:  or trusted the repository.\n51-- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n52:  global policy.\n53-- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n54-- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n55-  isolation.\n56-- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n57-  affected behavior.\n58-\n59-## Non-goals\n60-\n61:- A CLI editor for global settings.\n62:- A global-enablement or trust wizard.\n63:- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n64-  and deduplication contract.\n65-- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n66-  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n67-- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n--\n72-## User contract\n73-\n74-### Global settings are file-only\n75-\n76:The `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n77-\n78-```json\n79-{\n80:  \"global\": {\n81-    \"enabled\": true,\n82-    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n83-    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n84-    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n85:    \"trust_all\": false,\n86:    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n87:    \"trusted_paths\": []\n88-  }\n89-}\n90-```\n91-\n92-The refactor removes every CLI mutation path for this block:\n93-\n94:- `entire enable --global`;\n95:- `entire disable --global`;\n96:- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question;\n97:- `entire trust` and `entire trust --revoke`; and\n98:- the interactive pre-push trust prompt, including the \"Always\" writer.\n99-\n100-Status, doctor, and hold messages may show the resolved settings path and the\n101:specific keys a user needs to edit. They must not mutate the global block.\n102-\n103:### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n104-\n105:Entire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n106:(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\n107-and the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\n108:missing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n109-\n110:The installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\n111:policy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\n112-tracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n113-\n114:Hook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\n115-a supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\n116-integration-changing command or doctor repair.\n117-\n118-## Repository policy\n--\n138-\n139-Exact names may follow local conventions. `ClassifyRepoPolicy` never writes and\n140-may return a proposed layout when no route exists. Status, doctor, help, auth,\n141-and other read-only commands use this result directly and create no marker,\n142:directory, hook, or settings file.\n143-\n144-Runtime-writing callers use a separate `EnsureRuntimeRoute` operation. It\n145-atomically establishes the proposed route, rereads the winner of any race, and\n146-returns a new immutable policy snapshot containing the selected route. That\n147:snapshot is passed through context to hook dispatch, lazy setup, logging, and\n148-egress paths. Consumers must not reconstruct policy from overlapping boolean\n149-helpers or continue using the pre-establishment snapshot.\n150-\n151-This leaf package owns:\n152-\n153:- user-global settings loading needed by the gate;\n154-- repository activation-marker loading;\n155-- strict repository-settings provenance checks;\n156-- origin and path identity normalization;\n157:- exclusion and trust matching; and\n158-- runtime-route marker resolution.\n159-\n160-The `settings` package remains the public typed configuration API. The `paths`\n161-package constructs paths from a resolved layout and does not read or decode\n--\n164-### Activation provenance\n165-\n166-Repository content is configuration, not local activation evidence.\n167-`<worktree>/.entire/settings.json` containing `\"enabled\": true` does not by\n168:itself activate user-level hooks and never grants egress consent.\n169-\n170-Ordinary `entire enable` records a machine-local activation marker beneath the\n171-current worktree's namespace in the Git common directory. The marker is not\n172-cloned, committed, or shared between linked worktrees.\n173-\n174:Legacy bootstrap is deliberately narrower than “an Entire Git hook exists.” A\n175-single-worktree repository may bootstrap from an enabled repository setting plus\n176:an Entire hook installed inside its actual Git directory, because that hook\n177:cannot arrive in a clone. Linked worktrees share the common hooks directory, so\n178:common-hook presence is never worktree-specific evidence there. A linked\n179-worktree bootstraps only from verified untracked, non-symlinked runtime evidence\n180-inside that exact worktree or from an explicit `entire enable` run in that\n181:worktree. A tracked `core.hooksPath` inside the worktree is never activation\n182-evidence.\n183-\n184-Accepted worktree-specific legacy evidence is intentionally exact:\n185-\n--\n189-  `.entire/metadata/<session-id>/` (`prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `full.log`,\n190-  `transcript.jsonl`, `metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`, or\n191-  `content_hash.txt`).\n192-\n193:Empty directories, logs, tmp files, project settings, shared/common hooks, and\n194:any external or worktree-relative `core.hooksPath` are not bootstrap evidence.\n195-\n196-A repository-level `enabled: false` may remain an explicit capture veto. An\n197:`enabled: true` value configures an already locally or globally activated\n198-repository but does not create activation.\n199-\n200-### Settings provenance and executable values\n201-\n202-Hook-time settings are exposed through a sanitized view selected by activation\n203-source. Foreground commands unrelated to capture retain their current settings\n204:behavior; this matrix governs agent hooks, Git hooks, checkpoint creation, and\n205-checkpoint egress.\n206-\n207-| Settings field/group | Global-only activation | Local activation |\n208-| --- | --- | --- |\n--\n211-| Built-in declarative redaction (`pii`, `custom_redactions`, `externalize_images`) | Allowed; malformed values fail closed | Existing merged behavior |\n212-| External scanner/OPF configuration (`openai_privacy_filter`, `betterleaks`, `goredact`) | Ignored; compiled safe defaults only | Existing behavior, including the rule that `opf_command` must come from verified untracked `settings.local.json` |\n213-| `strategy_options`, including `checkpoint_remote` | Ignored; default checkpoint strategy and destination derivation | Existing behavior and inherited-remote ownership checks |\n214-| `checkpoints`, `sign_checkpoint_commits`, `vercel` | Ignored | Existing behavior |\n215:| `absolute_git_hook_path`, `external_agents` | Ignored; global infrastructure uses built-in supported agents only | Existing behavior |\n216-| `commit_linking` | Ignored; compiled default | Existing behavior |\n217:| Telemetry, review, investigate, and summary-generation settings | Not consumed by the hook-time sanitized view | Unchanged for their existing foreground consumers |\n218-\n219-The whitelist is implemented positively; adding a new `EntireSettings` field\n220:does not automatically make it available to global-only hooks. A test must\n221-classify every new field before the sanitized-view switch can expose it.\n222-\n223-Local settings paths are rejected as locally owned if the file or any parent\n224-path component traverses a symlink. Tracked, unreadable, malformed, or\n225:unverifiable local configuration fails toward the untrusted classification.\n226-Local activation does not relax existing per-field provenance checks: in\n227-particular, a project-file `opf_command` remains rejected after `entire enable`.\n228-Symlink validation starts from the canonical worktree root and inspects only the\n229-worktree-relative `.entire/settings*.json` components with `lstat`; it does not\n--\n244-\n245-- an existing explicitly enabled repository with local `.entire` runtime data\n246-  keeps the worktree layout;\n247-- a new `entire enable` with no prior route selects the worktree layout when\n248:  global policy is inactive, or the git-common layout when global policy is\n249-  already active;\n250:- a repository first activated by user-global policy selects the hidden\n251-  git-common-dir layout; and\n252:- later transitions between global and local activation do not move runtime\n253-  data or change the selected route.\n254-\n255-Route and activation records always live in a per-worktree registry beneath the\n256-clone's Git common directory:\n--\n288-\n289-### Atomic route selection\n290-\n291-The first process that needs runtime storage establishes the route before writing\n292:runtime data. Read-only commands never call this operation. Competing global-hook\n293-and `entire enable` processes use a short, bounded route-selection critical\n294-section:\n295-\n296-1. derive the deterministic per-worktree marker path;\n297-2. acquire the per-worktree route-selection lock only for read/create/publish;\n298-3. write and fsync a temporary complete marker, then atomically publish it\n299-   without replacing an existing marker;\n300-4. on collision, read and use the winner; and\n301:5. on an unverifiable result, skip hook capture rather than guessing a route.\n302-\n303:The proposal is deterministic for the normal race: while global policy is\n304:active, both a first global hook and concurrent `entire enable` propose the\n305:git-common layout. With global policy inactive, explicit enable proposes the\n306-worktree layout. A conflicting proposal caused by a concurrent settings edit is\n307-resolved by first published route wins.\n308-\n309-No long-held migration lock is needed. Hooks do not block behind interactive\n310-input or condensation, and lock failure cannot fail a Git commit, push, or agent\n311-session.\n312-\n313-The existing takeover planner, recursive mover, transcript-path repair,\n314:migration gate, retry marker, and test-only migration wrappers are removed.\n315-\n316-### Activation and route transitions\n317-\n318-| Starting state and action | Result |\n319-| --- | --- |\n320:| No markers, global off | Inactive; read-only commands make no writes |\n321:| No markers, global on; first hook | Select git-common route, leave local activation absent, capture globally |\n322:| No markers, global off; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select worktree route, then atomically set local activation `enabled` |\n323:| No markers, global on; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select git-common route, then set local activation `enabled` |\n324-| Existing route; `entire enable` | Preserve route and set local activation `enabled` |\n325:| `entire disable` | Set local activation `disabled`, preserve route and runtime data; this is a per-worktree veto over global activation. A global-only repo writes no worktree file; a locally activated repo also preserves the existing repo-settings disable behavior |\n326-| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n327:| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n328-| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n329:| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n330-| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n331-| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n332-| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n333-\n334-If repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n335:`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\n336:inactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\n337-policy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n338-\n339:## Egress trust\n340-\n341-### File-defined consent\n342-\n343:For a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\n344:active global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n345-\n346:- `global.trust_all` is true;\n347-- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n348:  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n349:- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n350-\n351-All errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\n352:normalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\n353:URL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\n354:fallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\n355:inactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\n356-when capture is inactive.\n357-\n358:Because the CLI no longer mutates trust entries, there is no asymmetric identity\n359-pruning or revoke operation. A stale origin entry remains explicit user\n360-configuration; removing that entry from the settings file is the revocation.\n361:Status reports the exact currently matched trust source.\n362-\n363-Locally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\n364-behavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\n365-inferred from committed settings.\n366-\n367-### Push behavior\n368-\n369:Pre-push never prompts. If an untrusted globally activated repository has no\n370:pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing. If checkpoints are pending, the\n371:hook prints one single-line hold notice, returns success, and leaves the user's\n372-normal branch push untouched. The notice points to the resolved user settings\n373-file instead of an unavailable command.\n374-\n375-Every checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\n376:Doctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\n377-gate merely because it is interactive.\n378-\n379:## Agent hook model\n380-\n381:Each supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n382-\n383-- installation;\n384-- recognition and version checks;\n385-- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n386-- removal of stale Entire entries;\n387-- expected counts; and\n388-- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n389-\n390:Gemini hooks whose parser always returns no lifecycle event are not installed:\n391-`AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`, and\n392-`Notification`.\n393:Legacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\n394-reconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\n395-commands.\n396-\n397:An agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\n398-lazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\n399-settings or re-running Git probes.\n400-\n401-### Hook reconciliation operations\n--\n408-- `entire enable`;\n409-- `entire agent add`; and\n410-- doctor's confirmed repair path.\n411-\n412:It does not run from hooks or read-only commands such as `status`, `help`,\n413-`version`, `auth token`, or JSON inspection commands. Reconciliation targets\n414-only supported agents positively detected as installed, or agents whose user\n415-configuration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\n416-only. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\n417-repo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n418-\n419-Each agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\n420-read-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\n421:enable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\n422:doctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\n423-process or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n424-\n425-Lazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\n426:current Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n427:`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\n428-doctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\n429:make a later hook repair it.\n430-\n431-## Status, doctor, and errors\n432-\n433-Status reports:\n434-\n435:- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n436-- the resolved settings-file path;\n437-- activation source for the current repository;\n438-- runtime layout;\n439:- trust source or held state; and\n440:- user-hook installation health.\n441-\n442:Doctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\n443:the global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\n444:settings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n445-\n446:Policy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\n447-migration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\n448-user's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\n449-aborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\n450-behavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\n451:other established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\n452-to fail that push.\n453-\n454-## Findings disposition\n455-\n456-| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n457-| --- | --- | --- |\n458:| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove migration gate and interactive trust prompt | Pending and non-pending pre-push cases never read stdin; non-pending is silent; pending emits one line; concurrent hook and push do not block |\n459:| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n460:| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n461:| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n462:| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Remove pre-push trust prompt | `ACCESSIBLE=1` pre-push remains non-interactive, held, and panic-free |\n463:| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n464:| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Remove trust writers/revoke; use pure current-identity matching over explicit settings | Origin→path→origin and multiple fetch/push URL cases authorize only when every current key is explicitly trusted |\n465:| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n466-\n467:Two older GitHub comments are also covered: the removed prompt can no longer\n468-misstate folder-versus-origin scope, and the remaining hold message is exactly\n469-one line.\n470-\n471-## Testing strategy\n--\n473-### Policy matrix\n474-\n475-Table-driven tests cover combinations of:\n476-\n477:- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n478-- local activation marker absent or present;\n479-- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n480-- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n481-- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n482:- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n483-\n484-Every case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\n485:decision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n486-\n487-### Runtime and concurrency\n488-\n489-- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n490:- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n491:- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n492-- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n493-  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n494-  recorded independently.\n495-- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n496-- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n497-  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n498-- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n499:  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n500:- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n501:  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n502-  in both cases.\n503-- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n504-- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n505-  because those components no longer exist.\n506-\n507-### Hooks and security\n508-\n509:- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n510:- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n511:- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n512:- Gemini no-op lifecycle hooks are absent.\n513-- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n514-  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n515-- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n516-  malicious committed-value test.\n517:- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n518:  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n519:- Accessible mode cannot grant consent or consume pre-push stdin because the\n520-  prompt is absent.\n521-- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n522:  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n523-\n524-### Integration\n525-\n526-Real-binary integration tests cover:\n527-\n528:1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n529:2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> successful branch push plus one-line hold;\n530:3. settings-file origin/path trust -> queued checkpoint drain;\n531:4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n532-   session visibility;\n533-5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n534-   behavior; and\n535-6. linked-worktree isolation.\n--\n545-commits:\n546-\n547-1. policy and provenance model;\n548-2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n549:3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n550:4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n551-5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n552-6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n553-\n554-PR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\n--\n564-\n565-### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n566-\n567-This is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n568:long-lived hook locks, route-transition races, and most of the current test\n569-surface. It does not address the architectural cause of the findings.\n570-\n571-### Move every repository to git-side runtime storage\n572-\n573-A universal git-common-dir runtime is the cleanest abstract model, but it would\n574-force a migration on every existing explicitly enabled repository and change\n575-established `.entire/logs`, `metadata`, and `tmp` behavior. That expansion is not\n576:required to make the unmerged global feature coherent.\n577-\n578:### Keep interactive trust while making activation settings-only\n579-\n580-This retains a convenient consent prompt but violates the requirement that the\n581:global block is writable only through the settings file. It also preserves the\n582:accessible-mode stdin hazard and keeps human input inside pre-push execution.\n583-\n584-### Infer local activation from committed project settings\n585-\n586-This preserves the current short circuit but lets cloned content grant egress\n587-and unlock executable configuration. Machine-local activation evidence is the\n588:necessary security boundary once user-level hooks are installed everywhere.\n"},"started_at_ms":1787607912186,"completed_at_ms":1787607912186}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:45:12.244Z","ordinal":9618,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a035bc-0f34-7791-b852-868e42c8b8a5","call_id":"call_7JFHCJiRsn07E3rwvglEQn6W","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"fbec35\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000003125,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6900,\"output\":\"1-# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\\n2-\\n3-**Status:** Approved for implementation planning  \\n4-**Date:** 2026-08-24  \\n5:**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030  \\n6:**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\\n7-\\n8-## Context\\n9-\\n10:The consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\\n11-\\n12:1. user-global settings and hook gates;\\n13-2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\\n14:3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\\n15:4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\\n16:5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\\n17-\\n18-The combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\\n19-the APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\\n20-activation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\\n21:agent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\\n22-must participate in.\\n23-\\n24-The desired product contract is now narrower:\\n25-\\n26:- all global tracking configuration is edited only in the user's\\n27-  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\\n28-  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\\n29:- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that mutate the `global`\\n30-  settings block;\\n31:- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\\n32-  inert until repository policy activates them; and\\n33-- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\\n34-\\n35-Trail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\\n--\\n37-transitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\\n38-\\n39-## Goals\\n40-\\n41:- Make the user settings file the sole write surface for global activation,\\n42:  exclusions, and trust.\\n43:- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\\n44-  policy.\\n45:- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\\n46-  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\\n47-- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\\n48-  process through one typed policy result.\\n49-- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\\n50:  or trusted the repository.\\n51-- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\\n52:  global policy.\\n53-- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\\n54-- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\\n55-  isolation.\\n56-- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\\n57-  affected behavior.\\n58-\\n59-## Non-goals\\n60-\\n61:- A CLI editor for global settings.\\n62:- A global-enablement or trust wizard.\\n63:- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\\n64-  and deduplication contract.\\n65-- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\\n66-  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\\n67-- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\\n--\\n72-## User contract\\n73-\\n74-### Global settings are file-only\\n75-\\n76:The `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\\n77-\\n78-```json\\n79-{\\n80:  \\\"global\\\": {\\n81-    \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n82-    \\\"exclude_paths\\\": [],\\n83-    \\\"exclude_paths_exact\\\": [],\\n84-    \\\"exclude_origins\\\": [],\\n85:    \\\"trust_all\\\": false,\\n86:    \\\"trusted_origins\\\": [],\\n87:    \\\"trusted_paths\\\": []\\n88-  }\\n89-}\\n90-```\\n91-\\n92-The refactor removes every CLI mutation path for this block:\\n93-\\n94:- `entire enable --global`;\\n95:- `entire disable --global`;\\n96:- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question;\\n97:- `entire trust` and `entire trust --revoke`; and\\n98:- the interactive pre-push trust prompt, including the \\\"Always\\\" writer.\\n99-\\n100-Status, doctor, and hold messages may show the resolved settings path and the\\n101:specific keys a user needs to edit. They must not mutate the global block.\\n102-\\n103:### User-level hooks are infrastructure\\n104-\\n105:Entire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\\n106:(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\\n107-and the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\\n108:missing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\\n109-\\n110:The installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\\n111:policy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\\n112-tracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\\n113-\\n114:Hook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\\n115-a supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\\n116-integration-changing command or doctor repair.\\n117-\\n118-## Repository policy\\n--\\n138-\\n139-Exact names may follow local conventions. `ClassifyRepoPolicy` never writes and\\n140-may return a proposed layout when no route exists. Status, doctor, help, auth,\\n141-and other read-only commands use this result directly and create no marker,\\n142:directory, hook, or settings file.\\n143-\\n144-Runtime-writing callers use a separate `EnsureRuntimeRoute` operation. It\\n145-atomically establishes the proposed route, rereads the winner of any race, and\\n146-returns a new immutable policy snapshot containing the selected route. That\\n147:snapshot is passed through context to hook dispatch, lazy setup, logging, and\\n148-egress paths. Consumers must not reconstruct policy from overlapping boolean\\n149-helpers or continue using the pre-establishment snapshot.\\n150-\\n151-This leaf package owns:\\n152-\\n153:- user-global settings loading needed by the gate;\\n154-- repository activation-marker loading;\\n155-- strict repository-settings provenance checks;\\n156-- origin and path identity normalization;\\n157:- exclusion and trust matching; and\\n158-- runtime-route marker resolution.\\n159-\\n160-The `settings` package remains the public typed configuration API. The `paths`\\n161-package constructs paths from a resolved layout and does not read or decode\\n--\\n164-### Activation provenance\\n165-\\n166-Repository content is configuration, not local activation evidence.\\n167-`<worktree>/.entire/settings.json` containing `\\\"enabled\\\": true` does not by\\n168:itself activate user-level hooks and never grants egress consent.\\n169-\\n170-Ordinary `entire enable` records a machine-local activation marker beneath the\\n171-current worktree's namespace in the Git common directory. The marker is not\\n172-cloned, committed, or shared between linked worktrees.\\n173-\\n174:Legacy bootstrap is deliberately narrower than “an Entire Git hook exists.” A\\n175-single-worktree repository may bootstrap from an enabled repository setting plus\\n176:an Entire hook installed inside its actual Git directory, because that hook\\n177:cannot arrive in a clone. Linked worktrees share the common hooks directory, so\\n178:common-hook presence is never worktree-specific evidence there. A linked\\n179-worktree bootstraps only from verified untracked, non-symlinked runtime evidence\\n180-inside that exact worktree or from an explicit `entire enable` run in that\\n181:worktree. A tracked `core.hooksPath` inside the worktree is never activation\\n182-evidence.\\n183-\\n184-Accepted worktree-specific legacy evidence is intentionally exact:\\n185-\\n--\\n189-  `.entire/metadata/<session-id>/` (`prompt.txt`, `full.jsonl`, `full.log`,\\n190-  `transcript.jsonl`, `metadata.json`, `checkpoint.json`, or\\n191-  `content_hash.txt`).\\n192-\\n193:Empty directories, logs, tmp files, project settings, shared/common hooks, and\\n194:any external or worktree-relative `core.hooksPath` are not bootstrap evidence.\\n195-\\n196-A repository-level `enabled: false` may remain an explicit capture veto. An\\n197:`enabled: true` value configures an already locally or globally activated\\n198-repository but does not create activation.\\n199-\\n200-### Settings provenance and executable values\\n201-\\n202-Hook-time settings are exposed through a sanitized view selected by activation\\n203-source. Foreground commands unrelated to capture retain their current settings\\n204:behavior; this matrix governs agent hooks, Git hooks, checkpoint creation, and\\n205-checkpoint egress.\\n206-\\n207-| Settings field/group | Global-only activation | Local activation |\\n208-| --- | --- | --- |\\n--\\n211-| Built-in declarative redaction (`pii`, `custom_redactions`, `externalize_images`) | Allowed; malformed values fail closed | Existing merged behavior |\\n212-| External scanner/OPF configuration (`openai_privacy_filter`, `betterleaks`, `goredact`) | Ignored; compiled safe defaults only | Existing behavior, including the rule that `opf_command` must come from verified untracked `settings.local.json` |\\n213-| `strategy_options`, including `checkpoint_remote` | Ignored; default checkpoint strategy and destination derivation | Existing behavior and inherited-remote ownership checks |\\n214-| `checkpoints`, `sign_checkpoint_commits`, `vercel` | Ignored | Existing behavior |\\n215:| `absolute_git_hook_path`, `external_agents` | Ignored; global infrastructure uses built-in supported agents only | Existing behavior |\\n216-| `commit_linking` | Ignored; compiled default | Existing behavior |\\n217:| Telemetry, review, investigate, and summary-generation settings | Not consumed by the hook-time sanitized view | Unchanged for their existing foreground consumers |\\n218-\\n219-The whitelist is implemented positively; adding a new `EntireSettings` field\\n220:does not automatically make it available to global-only hooks. A test must\\n221-classify every new field before the sanitized-view switch can expose it.\\n222-\\n223-Local settings paths are rejected as locally owned if the file or any parent\\n224-path component traverses a symlink. Tracked, unreadable, malformed, or\\n225:unverifiable local configuration fails toward the untrusted classification.\\n226-Local activation does not relax existing per-field provenance checks: in\\n227-particular, a project-file `opf_command` remains rejected after `entire enable`.\\n228-Symlink validation starts from the canonical worktree root and inspects only the\\n229-worktree-relative `.entire/settings*.json` components with `lstat`; it does not\\n--\\n244-\\n245-- an existing explicitly enabled repository with local `.entire` runtime data\\n246-  keeps the worktree layout;\\n247-- a new `entire enable` with no prior route selects the worktree layout when\\n248:  global policy is inactive, or the git-common layout when global policy is\\n249-  already active;\\n250:- a repository first activated by user-global policy selects the hidden\\n251-  git-common-dir layout; and\\n252:- later transitions between global and local activation do not move runtime\\n253-  data or change the selected route.\\n254-\\n255-Route and activation records always live in a per-worktree registry beneath the\\n256-clone's Git common directory:\\n--\\n288-\\n289-### Atomic route selection\\n290-\\n291-The first process that needs runtime storage establishes the route before writing\\n292:runtime data. Read-only commands never call this operation. Competing global-hook\\n293-and `entire enable` processes use a short, bounded route-selection critical\\n294-section:\\n295-\\n296-1. derive the deterministic per-worktree marker path;\\n297-2. acquire the per-worktree route-selection lock only for read/create/publish;\\n298-3. write and fsync a temporary complete marker, then atomically publish it\\n299-   without replacing an existing marker;\\n300-4. on collision, read and use the winner; and\\n301:5. on an unverifiable result, skip hook capture rather than guessing a route.\\n302-\\n303:The proposal is deterministic for the normal race: while global policy is\\n304:active, both a first global hook and concurrent `entire enable` propose the\\n305:git-common layout. With global policy inactive, explicit enable proposes the\\n306-worktree layout. A conflicting proposal caused by a concurrent settings edit is\\n307-resolved by first published route wins.\\n308-\\n309-No long-held migration lock is needed. Hooks do not block behind interactive\\n310-input or condensation, and lock failure cannot fail a Git commit, push, or agent\\n311-session.\\n312-\\n313-The existing takeover planner, recursive mover, transcript-path repair,\\n314:migration gate, retry marker, and test-only migration wrappers are removed.\\n315-\\n316-### Activation and route transitions\\n317-\\n318-| Starting state and action | Result |\\n319-| --- | --- |\\n320:| No markers, global off | Inactive; read-only commands make no writes |\\n321:| No markers, global on; first hook | Select git-common route, leave local activation absent, capture globally |\\n322:| No markers, global off; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select worktree route, then atomically set local activation `enabled` |\\n323:| No markers, global on; `entire enable` | Persist repo settings, select git-common route, then set local activation `enabled` |\\n324-| Existing route; `entire enable` | Preserve route and set local activation `enabled` |\\n325:| `entire disable` | Set local activation `disabled`, preserve route and runtime data; this is a per-worktree veto over global activation. A global-only repo writes no worktree file; a locally activated repo also preserves the existing repo-settings disable behavior |\\n326-| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\\n327:| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\\n328-| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\\n329:| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\\n330-| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\\n331-| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\\n332-| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\\n333-\\n334-If repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\\n335:`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\\n336:inactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\\n337-policy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\\n338-\\n339:## Egress trust\\n340-\\n341-### File-defined consent\\n342-\\n343:For a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\\n344:active global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\\n345-\\n346:- `global.trust_all` is true;\\n347-- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\\n348:  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\\n349:- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\\n350-\\n351-All errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\\n352:normalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\\n353:URL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\\n354:fallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\\n355:inactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\\n356-when capture is inactive.\\n357-\\n358:Because the CLI no longer mutates trust entries, there is no asymmetric identity\\n359-pruning or revoke operation. A stale origin entry remains explicit user\\n360-configuration; removing that entry from the settings file is the revocation.\\n361:Status reports the exact currently matched trust source.\\n362-\\n363-Locally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\\n364-behavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\\n365-inferred from committed settings.\\n366-\\n367-### Push behavior\\n368-\\n369:Pre-push never prompts. If an untrusted globally activated repository has no\\n370:pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing. If checkpoints are pending, the\\n371:hook prints one single-line hold notice, returns success, and leaves the user's\\n372-normal branch push untouched. The notice points to the resolved user settings\\n373-file instead of an unavailable command.\\n374-\\n375-Every checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\\n376:Doctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\\n377-gate merely because it is interactive.\\n378-\\n379:## Agent hook model\\n380-\\n381:Each supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\\n382-\\n383-- installation;\\n384-- recognition and version checks;\\n385-- deduplication between user and repository scope;\\n386-- removal of stale Entire entries;\\n387-- expected counts; and\\n388-- doctor/status completeness reporting.\\n389-\\n390:Gemini hooks whose parser always returns no lifecycle event are not installed:\\n391-`AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`, and\\n392-`Notification`.\\n393:Legacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\\n394-reconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\\n395-commands.\\n396-\\n397:An agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\\n398-lazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\\n399-settings or re-running Git probes.\\n400-\\n401-### Hook reconciliation operations\\n--\\n408-- `entire enable`;\\n409-- `entire agent add`; and\\n410-- doctor's confirmed repair path.\\n411-\\n412:It does not run from hooks or read-only commands such as `status`, `help`,\\n413-`version`, `auth token`, or JSON inspection commands. Reconciliation targets\\n414-only supported agents positively detected as installed, or agents whose user\\n415-configuration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\\n416-only. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\\n417-repo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\\n418-\\n419-Each agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\\n420-read-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\\n421:enable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\\n422:doctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\\n423-process or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\\n424-\\n425-Lazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\\n426:current Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\\n427:`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\\n428-doctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\\n429:make a later hook repair it.\\n430-\\n431-## Status, doctor, and errors\\n432-\\n433-Status reports:\\n434-\\n435:- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\\n436-- the resolved settings-file path;\\n437-- activation source for the current repository;\\n438-- runtime layout;\\n439:- trust source or held state; and\\n440:- user-hook installation health.\\n441-\\n442:Doctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\\n443:the global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\\n444:settings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\\n445-\\n446:Policy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\\n447-migration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\\n448-user's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\\n449-aborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\\n450-behavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\\n451:other established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\\n452-to fail that push.\\n453-\\n454-## Findings disposition\\n455-\\n456-| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\\n457-| --- | --- | --- |\\n458:| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove migration gate and interactive trust prompt | Pending and non-pending pre-push cases never read stdin; non-pending is silent; pending emits one line; concurrent hook and push do not block |\\n459:| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\\n460:| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\\n461:| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\\n462:| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Remove pre-push trust prompt | `ACCESSIBLE=1` pre-push remains non-interactive, held, and panic-free |\\n463:| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\\n464:| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Remove trust writers/revoke; use pure current-identity matching over explicit settings | Origin→path→origin and multiple fetch/push URL cases authorize only when every current key is explicitly trusted |\\n465:| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\\n466-\\n467:Two older GitHub comments are also covered: the removed prompt can no longer\\n468-misstate folder-versus-origin scope, and the remaining hold message is exactly\\n469-one line.\\n470-\\n471-## Testing strategy\\n--\\n473-### Policy matrix\\n474-\\n475-Table-driven tests cover combinations of:\\n476-\\n477:- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\\n478-- local activation marker absent or present;\\n479-- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\\n480-- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\\n481-- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\\n482:- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\\n483-\\n484-Every case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\\n485:decision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\\n486-\\n487-### Runtime and concurrency\\n488-\\n489-- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\\n490:- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\\n491:- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\\n492-- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\\n493-  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\\n494-  recorded independently.\\n495-- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\\n496-- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\\n497-  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\\n498-- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\\n499:  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\\n500:- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\\n501:  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\\n502-  in both cases.\\n503-- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\\n504-- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\\n505-  because those components no longer exist.\\n506-\\n507-### Hooks and security\\n508-\\n509:- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\\n510:- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\\n511:- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\\n512:- Gemini no-op lifecycle hooks are absent.\\n513-- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\\n514-  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\\n515-- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\\n516-  malicious committed-value test.\\n517:- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\\n518:  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\\n519:- Accessible mode cannot grant consent or consume pre-push stdin because the\\n520-  prompt is absent.\\n521-- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\\n522:  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\\n523-\\n524-### Integration\\n525-\\n526-Real-binary integration tests cover:\\n527-\\n528:1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\\n529:2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> successful branch push plus one-line hold;\\n530:3. settings-file origin/path trust -> queued checkpoint drain;\\n531:4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\\n532-   session visibility;\\n533-5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\\n534-   behavior; and\\n535-6. linked-worktree isolation.\\n--\\n545-commits:\\n546-\\n547-1. policy and provenance model;\\n548-2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\\n549:3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\\n550:4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\\n551-5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\\n552-6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\\n553-\\n554-PR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\\n--\\n564-\\n565-### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\\n566-\\n567-This is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\\n568:long-lived hook locks, route-transition races, and most of the current test\\n569-surface. It does not address the architectural cause of the findings.\\n570-\\n571-### Move every repository to git-side runtime storage\\n572-\\n573-A universal git-common-dir runtime is the cleanest abstract model, but it would\\n574-force a migration on every existing explicitly enabled repository and change\\n575-established `.entire/logs`, `metadata`, and `tmp` behavior. That expansion is not\\n576:required to make the unmerged global feature coherent.\\n577-\\n578:### Keep interactive trust while making activation settings-only\\n579-\\n580-This retains a convenient consent prompt but violates the requirement that the\\n581:global block is writable only through the settings file. It also preserves the\\n582:accessible-mode stdin hazard and keeps human input inside pre-push execution.\\n583-\\n584-### Infer local activation from committed project settings\\n585-\\n586-This preserves the current short circuit but lets cloned content grant egress\\n587-and unlock executable configuration. Machine-local activation evidence is the\\n588:necessary security boundary once user-level hooks are installed everywhere.\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","create_time":1787607912.244689}}}
