{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:47:00.052Z","ordinal":9704,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_068dffc2438c6825016a8cbbd22be087d0b10888e542521b1a","status":"completed","call_id":"call_vY05i69M0UJJxSGloIAH9qEO","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,125p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md && sed -n '335,445p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md && sed -n '455,610p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/Documents - Peyton’s MacBook Pro/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/global-settings-tier\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","create_time":1787608017.151457}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:47:00.128Z","ordinal":9705,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a025ce-e754-7631-8467-eaa6035ba0f8","turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-34ea7e58-08e1-4dd7-83d9-cf6129fb4e47","process_id":"24778","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,125p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md && sed -n '335,445p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md && sed -n '455,610p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/peytonmontei/Documents/Documents%20-%20Peyton%E2%80%99s%20MacBook%20Pro/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/global-settings-tier","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,125p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","name":"2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '335,445p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","name":"2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '455,610p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","name":"2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md","path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-settings-only-global-tracking-refactor-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n\n**Status:** Revised after product-behavior clarification; awaiting approval\n**Date:** 2026-08-24\n**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030\n**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n\n## Context\n\nThe consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n\n1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n\nThe combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\nthe APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\nactivation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\nagent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\nmust participate in.\n\nThe desired product contract is now narrower:\n\n- global activation and exclusion policy are edited in the user's\n  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that turn global tracking\n  on or off;\n- the existing pre-push trust prompt, `entire trust`/`--revoke`, and\n  settings-backed egress gate remain product behavior;\n- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n  inert until repository policy activates them, without changing Claude Code\n  or Gemini CLI event coverage; and\n- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n\nTrail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\nlow. This design treats them as acceptance criteria. It removes the unsafe state\ntransitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Make the user settings file the sole direct configuration surface for global\n  activation and exclusions while preserving the existing trust UX and its\n  settings-backed consent writes.\n- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n  policy.\n- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n  process through one typed policy result.\n- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n  or trusted the repository.\n- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n  global policy.\n- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n  isolation.\n- Preserve the current pre-push trust choices, egress hold/drain behavior, and\n  Claude Code/Gemini CLI lifecycle coverage.\n- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n  affected behavior.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- A CLI editor for global settings.\n- A global-enablement wizard.\n- Redesigning the pre-push trust experience, `entire trust`, checkpoint egress,\n  or the supported Claude Code/Gemini CLI event surfaces.\n- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n  and deduplication contract.\n- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n  storage.\n- Running real-agent E2E tests unless explicitly requested under repository\n  policy.\n\n## User contract\n\n### Global activation is file-only\n\nThe `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"global\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n    \"trust_all\": false,\n    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n    \"trusted_paths\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe refactor removes the CLI mutation paths for global activation:\n\n- `entire enable --global`;\n- `entire disable --global`;\n- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question.\n\n`entire trust`, `entire trust --revoke`, and the interactive pre-push choices\nremain. They may update only egress-consent fields (`trust_all`,\n`trusted_origins`, and `trusted_paths`) in the same user settings file; they do\nnot enable global tracking or change exclusions. Status and doctor continue to\nreport both activation and trust state.\n\n### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n\nEntire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\nand the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\nmissing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n\nThe installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\npolicy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\ntracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n\nHook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\na supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\nintegration-changing command or doctor repair.\n| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n\nIf repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\ninactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\npolicy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n\n## Egress trust\n\n### Settings-backed consent\n\nFor a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\nactive global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n\n- `global.trust_all` is true;\n- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n\nAll errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\nnormalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\nURL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\nfallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\ninactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\nwhen capture is inactive.\n\nThe existing consent surfaces remain:\n\n- `entire trust` grants trust for the current normalized repository identity;\n- `entire trust --revoke` withdraws trust previously granted for that\n  repository;\n- the interactive pre-push prompt offers \"Yes\", \"Not now\", and \"Always\"; and\n- users may edit the same trust fields directly in the settings file.\n\nAll writers use one identity derivation and one atomic settings update. A small\nmachine-local trust-grant ledger records which origin/path keys a CLI or\npre-push grant wrote for each canonical worktree. Re-granting after an identity\nchange removes superseded keys from that worktree's prior grant before writing\nthe new identity; revoke removes both the current identity and every key in that\nrecord. Hand-authored entries not recorded as a grant are never heuristically\ndeleted. This preserves origin-wide trust while fixing origin-to-path and\npath-to-origin stale grants. Status reports the exact currently matched trust\nsource.\n\nLocally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\nbehavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\ninferred from committed settings.\n\n### Push behavior\n\nPre-push preserves the current trust flow. If an untrusted globally activated\nrepository has no pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing and does not\nprompt. If checkpoints are pending and a real interactive terminal is\navailable, the hook opens the three-choice trust prompt using `/dev/tty`, never\nGit's stdin:\n\n- \"Yes\" records the current repository identity and drains the queued\n  checkpoints after re-evaluating policy;\n- \"Not now\" keeps capturing locally and leaves the queue for a later push; and\n- \"Always\" records `trust_all`, re-evaluates policy, and drains the queue.\n\nIf the prompt is unavailable, aborted, or fails—or if persistence or policy\nre-evaluation fails—the checkpoint queue remains held while the user's normal\nbranch push continues. Accessible mode must use a supported explicit selection\npath or hold; it must never accept a default implicitly, panic, or consume\npre-push stdin. The held message remains actionable and points to `entire trust`\nand the resolved settings file.\n\nEvery checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\nDoctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\ngate merely because it is interactive.\n\n## Agent hook model\n\nEach supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n\n- installation;\n- recognition and version checks;\n- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n- removal of stale Entire entries;\n- expected counts; and\n- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n\nThe declarative specification preserves the complete existing event set and\ncommand behavior. In particular, Gemini continues installing all twelve current\nentries: `SessionStart`, both `SessionEnd` matchers, `BeforeAgent`, `AfterAgent`,\n`BeforeModel`, `AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`,\n`PreCompress`, and `Notification`. Events that currently parse to no lifecycle\nevent remain installed because their presence is part of the supported Gemini\nintegration contract. Claude Code likewise preserves its current lifecycle and\ntool-use matcher coverage. The refactor deduplicates how these entries are\ndeclared and reconciled; it does not prune or reinterpret them.\nLegacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\nreconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\ncommands.\n\nAn agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\nlazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\nsettings or re-running Git probes.\n\n### Hook reconciliation operations\n\nUser-scope reconciliation runs only from these integration-changing entry\npoints:\n\nconfiguration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\nonly. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\nrepo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n\nEach agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\nread-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\nenable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\ndoctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\nprocess or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n\nLazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\ncurrent Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\ndoctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\nmake a later hook repair it.\n\n## Status, doctor, and errors\n\nStatus reports:\n\n- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n- the resolved settings-file path;\n- activation source for the current repository;\n- runtime layout;\n- trust source or held state; and\n- user-hook installation health.\n\nDoctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\nthe global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\nsettings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n\nPolicy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\nmigration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\nuser's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\naborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\nbehavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\nother established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\nto fail that push.\n\n## Findings disposition\n\n| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove the takeover migration gate; keep the trust prompt outside route-selection locking | Non-pending pre-push is silent; pending interactive pre-push prompts through `/dev/tty`; concurrent hook and push do not block on route selection |\n| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Preserve the prompt but require an explicit supported selection; otherwise hold | `ACCESSIBLE=1` never grants the default implicitly, never consumes Git stdin, and never panics; explicit selections retain Yes/Not now/Always semantics |\n| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Preserve trust/revoke and record the exact keys written by each CLI/prompt grant so later re-grant/revoke can remove its stale identities without deleting hand-authored entries | Origin→path→origin, path→origin→path, and multiple fetch/push URL cases leave only the intended grant and revoke every recorded identity |\n| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n\nTwo older GitHub comments are also covered: the preserved prompt derives its\nfolder-versus-origin label from the same identity used by the gate and writer,\nand the hold message remains concise and actionable.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\n### Policy matrix\n\nTable-driven tests cover combinations of:\n\n- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n- local activation marker absent or present;\n- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n\nEvery case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\ndecision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n\n### Runtime and concurrency\n\n- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n  recorded independently.\n- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n  in both cases.\n- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n  because those components no longer exist.\n\n### Hooks and security\n\n- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n- Claude Code and all twelve current Gemini entries remain present with the same\n  command forms, matchers, deduplication, and event behavior.\n- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n  malicious committed-value test.\n- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n- Accessible mode cannot grant consent implicitly or consume pre-push stdin;\n  explicit Yes/Not now/Always choices retain their current effects.\n- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n\n### Integration\n\nReal-binary integration tests cover:\n\n1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> interactive Yes/Not now/Always behavior,\n   plus non-interactive successful branch push with held checkpoints;\n3. `entire trust`, pre-push trust, and settings-file origin/path trust -> queued\n   checkpoint drain;\n4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n   session visibility;\n5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n   behavior; and\n6. linked-worktree isolation.\n\nRun focused tests during development and `mise run check` before every commit.\nRun deterministic canaries as part of the repository check. Real-agent E2E tests\nremain opt-in and require explicit user authorization.\n\n## Landing strategy\n\nThe refactor is prepared from `main` as one coherent review unit, not as another\nlayer on the old stack. The implementation should be organized into logical\ncommits:\n\n1. policy and provenance model;\n2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n\nPR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\ncoherent final implementation rather than the current stacked merge history.\nBefore any remote update, lint and the full repository check must pass. After a\npush, all repository and PR gates must reach terminal status; risk/security\nmetrics are compared and every valid finding is addressed before completion.\n\nTrail findings are resolved only after the corresponding implementation and\nregression test are present on the trail branch.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n\nThis is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n\n**Status:** Revised after product-behavior clarification; awaiting approval\n**Date:** 2026-08-24\n**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030\n**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n\n## Context\n\nThe consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n\n1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n\nThe combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\nthe APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\nactivation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\nagent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\nmust participate in.\n\nThe desired product contract is now narrower:\n\n- global activation and exclusion policy are edited in the user's\n  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that turn global tracking\n  on or off;\n- the existing pre-push trust prompt, `entire trust`/`--revoke`, and\n  settings-backed egress gate remain product behavior;\n- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n  inert until repository policy activates them, without changing Claude Code\n  or Gemini CLI event coverage; and\n- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n\nTrail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\nlow. This design treats them as acceptance criteria. It removes the unsafe state\ntransitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Make the user settings file the sole direct configuration surface for global\n  activation and exclusions while preserving the existing trust UX and its\n  settings-backed consent writes.\n- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n  policy.\n- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n  process through one typed policy result.\n- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n  or trusted the repository.\n- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n  global policy.\n- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n  isolation.\n- Preserve the current pre-push trust choices, egress hold/drain behavior, and\n  Claude Code/Gemini CLI lifecycle coverage.\n- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n  affected behavior.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- A CLI editor for global settings.\n- A global-enablement wizard.\n- Redesigning the pre-push trust experience, `entire trust`, checkpoint egress,\n  or the supported Claude Code/Gemini CLI event surfaces.\n- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n  and deduplication contract.\n- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n  storage.\n- Running real-agent E2E tests unless explicitly requested under repository\n  policy.\n\n## User contract\n\n### Global activation is file-only\n\nThe `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"global\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n    \"trust_all\": false,\n    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n    \"trusted_paths\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe refactor removes the CLI mutation paths for global activation:\n\n- `entire enable --global`;\n- `entire disable --global`;\n- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question.\n\n`entire trust`, `entire trust --revoke`, and the interactive pre-push choices\nremain. They may update only egress-consent fields (`trust_all`,\n`trusted_origins`, and `trusted_paths`) in the same user settings file; they do\nnot enable global tracking or change exclusions. Status and doctor continue to\nreport both activation and trust state.\n\n### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n\nEntire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\nand the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\nmissing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n\nThe installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\npolicy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\ntracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n\nHook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\na supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\nintegration-changing command or doctor repair.\n| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n\nIf repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\ninactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\npolicy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n\n## Egress trust\n\n### Settings-backed consent\n\nFor a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\nactive global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n\n- `global.trust_all` is true;\n- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n\nAll errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\nnormalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\nURL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\nfallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\ninactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\nwhen capture is inactive.\n\nThe existing consent surfaces remain:\n\n- `entire trust` grants trust for the current normalized repository identity;\n- `entire trust --revoke` withdraws trust previously granted for that\n  repository;\n- the interactive pre-push prompt offers \"Yes\", \"Not now\", and \"Always\"; and\n- users may edit the same trust fields directly in the settings file.\n\nAll writers use one identity derivation and one atomic settings update. A small\nmachine-local trust-grant ledger records which origin/path keys a CLI or\npre-push grant wrote for each canonical worktree. Re-granting after an identity\nchange removes superseded keys from that worktree's prior grant before writing\nthe new identity; revoke removes both the current identity and every key in that\nrecord. Hand-authored entries not recorded as a grant are never heuristically\ndeleted. This preserves origin-wide trust while fixing origin-to-path and\npath-to-origin stale grants. Status reports the exact currently matched trust\nsource.\n\nLocally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\nbehavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\ninferred from committed settings.\n\n### Push behavior\n\nPre-push preserves the current trust flow. If an untrusted globally activated\nrepository has no pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing and does not\nprompt. If checkpoints are pending and a real interactive terminal is\navailable, the hook opens the three-choice trust prompt using `/dev/tty`, never\nGit's stdin:\n\n- \"Yes\" records the current repository identity and drains the queued\n  checkpoints after re-evaluating policy;\n- \"Not now\" keeps capturing locally and leaves the queue for a later push; and\n- \"Always\" records `trust_all`, re-evaluates policy, and drains the queue.\n\nIf the prompt is unavailable, aborted, or fails—or if persistence or policy\nre-evaluation fails—the checkpoint queue remains held while the user's normal\nbranch push continues. Accessible mode must use a supported explicit selection\npath or hold; it must never accept a default implicitly, panic, or consume\npre-push stdin. The held message remains actionable and points to `entire trust`\nand the resolved settings file.\n\nEvery checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\nDoctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\ngate merely because it is interactive.\n\n## Agent hook model\n\nEach supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n\n- installation;\n- recognition and version checks;\n- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n- removal of stale Entire entries;\n- expected counts; and\n- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n\nThe declarative specification preserves the complete existing event set and\ncommand behavior. In particular, Gemini continues installing all twelve current\nentries: `SessionStart`, both `SessionEnd` matchers, `BeforeAgent`, `AfterAgent`,\n`BeforeModel`, `AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`,\n`PreCompress`, and `Notification`. Events that currently parse to no lifecycle\nevent remain installed because their presence is part of the supported Gemini\nintegration contract. Claude Code likewise preserves its current lifecycle and\ntool-use matcher coverage. The refactor deduplicates how these entries are\ndeclared and reconciled; it does not prune or reinterpret them.\nLegacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\nreconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\ncommands.\n\nAn agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\nlazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\nsettings or re-running Git probes.\n\n### Hook reconciliation operations\n\nUser-scope reconciliation runs only from these integration-changing entry\npoints:\n\nconfiguration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\nonly. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\nrepo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n\nEach agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\nread-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\nenable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\ndoctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\nprocess or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n\nLazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\ncurrent Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\ndoctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\nmake a later hook repair it.\n\n## Status, doctor, and errors\n\nStatus reports:\n\n- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n- the resolved settings-file path;\n- activation source for the current repository;\n- runtime layout;\n- trust source or held state; and\n- user-hook installation health.\n\nDoctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\nthe global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\nsettings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n\nPolicy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\nmigration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\nuser's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\naborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\nbehavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\nother established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\nto fail that push.\n\n## Findings disposition\n\n| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove the takeover migration gate; keep the trust prompt outside route-selection locking | Non-pending pre-push is silent; pending interactive pre-push prompts through `/dev/tty`; concurrent hook and push do not block on route selection |\n| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Preserve the prompt but require an explicit supported selection; otherwise hold | `ACCESSIBLE=1` never grants the default implicitly, never consumes Git stdin, and never panics; explicit selections retain Yes/Not now/Always semantics |\n| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Preserve trust/revoke and record the exact keys written by each CLI/prompt grant so later re-grant/revoke can remove its stale identities without deleting hand-authored entries | Origin→path→origin, path→origin→path, and multiple fetch/push URL cases leave only the intended grant and revoke every recorded identity |\n| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n\nTwo older GitHub comments are also covered: the preserved prompt derives its\nfolder-versus-origin label from the same identity used by the gate and writer,\nand the hold message remains concise and actionable.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\n### Policy matrix\n\nTable-driven tests cover combinations of:\n\n- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n- local activation marker absent or present;\n- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n\nEvery case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\ndecision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n\n### Runtime and concurrency\n\n- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n  recorded independently.\n- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n  in both cases.\n- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n  because those components no longer exist.\n\n### Hooks and security\n\n- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n- Claude Code and all twelve current Gemini entries remain present with the same\n  command forms, matchers, deduplication, and event behavior.\n- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n  malicious committed-value test.\n- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n- Accessible mode cannot grant consent implicitly or consume pre-push stdin;\n  explicit Yes/Not now/Always choices retain their current effects.\n- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n\n### Integration\n\nReal-binary integration tests cover:\n\n1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> interactive Yes/Not now/Always behavior,\n   plus non-interactive successful branch push with held checkpoints;\n3. `entire trust`, pre-push trust, and settings-file origin/path trust -> queued\n   checkpoint drain;\n4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n   session visibility;\n5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n   behavior; and\n6. linked-worktree isolation.\n\nRun focused tests during development and `mise run check` before every commit.\nRun deterministic canaries as part of the repository check. Real-agent E2E tests\nremain opt-in and require explicit user authorization.\n\n## Landing strategy\n\nThe refactor is prepared from `main` as one coherent review unit, not as another\nlayer on the old stack. The implementation should be organized into logical\ncommits:\n\n1. policy and provenance model;\n2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n\nPR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\ncoherent final implementation rather than the current stacked merge history.\nBefore any remote update, lint and the full repository check must pass. After a\npush, all repository and PR gates must reach terminal status; risk/security\nmetrics are compared and every valid finding is addressed before completion.\n\nTrail findings are resolved only after the corresponding implementation and\nregression test are present on the trail branch.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n\nThis is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2417},"formatted_output":"# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\n\n**Status:** Revised after product-behavior clarification; awaiting approval\n**Date:** 2026-08-24\n**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030\n**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\n\n## Context\n\nThe consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\n\n1. user-global settings and hook gates;\n2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\n3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\n4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\n5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\n\nThe combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\nthe APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\nactivation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\nagent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\nmust participate in.\n\nThe desired product contract is now narrower:\n\n- global activation and exclusion policy are edited in the user's\n  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\n  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\n- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that turn global tracking\n  on or off;\n- the existing pre-push trust prompt, `entire trust`/`--revoke`, and\n  settings-backed egress gate remain product behavior;\n- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\n  inert until repository policy activates them, without changing Claude Code\n  or Gemini CLI event coverage; and\n- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\n\nTrail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\nlow. This design treats them as acceptance criteria. It removes the unsafe state\ntransitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Make the user settings file the sole direct configuration surface for global\n  activation and exclusions while preserving the existing trust UX and its\n  settings-backed consent writes.\n- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\n  policy.\n- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\n  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\n- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\n  process through one typed policy result.\n- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\n  or trusted the repository.\n- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\n  global policy.\n- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\n- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\n  isolation.\n- Preserve the current pre-push trust choices, egress hold/drain behavior, and\n  Claude Code/Gemini CLI lifecycle coverage.\n- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\n  affected behavior.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- A CLI editor for global settings.\n- A global-enablement wizard.\n- Redesigning the pre-push trust experience, `entire trust`, checkpoint egress,\n  or the supported Claude Code/Gemini CLI event surfaces.\n- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\n  and deduplication contract.\n- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\n  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\n- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\n  storage.\n- Running real-agent E2E tests unless explicitly requested under repository\n  policy.\n\n## User contract\n\n### Global activation is file-only\n\nThe `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"global\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"exclude_paths\": [],\n    \"exclude_paths_exact\": [],\n    \"exclude_origins\": [],\n    \"trust_all\": false,\n    \"trusted_origins\": [],\n    \"trusted_paths\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe refactor removes the CLI mutation paths for global activation:\n\n- `entire enable --global`;\n- `entire disable --global`;\n- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question.\n\n`entire trust`, `entire trust --revoke`, and the interactive pre-push choices\nremain. They may update only egress-consent fields (`trust_all`,\n`trusted_origins`, and `trusted_paths`) in the same user settings file; they do\nnot enable global tracking or change exclusions. Status and doctor continue to\nreport both activation and trust state.\n\n### User-level hooks are infrastructure\n\nEntire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\n(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\nand the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\nmissing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\n\nThe installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\npolicy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\ntracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\n\nHook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\na supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\nintegration-changing command or doctor repair.\n| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\n| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\n| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\n| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\n| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\n| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\n| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\n\nIf repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\n`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\ninactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\npolicy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\n\n## Egress trust\n\n### Settings-backed consent\n\nFor a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\nactive global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\n\n- `global.trust_all` is true;\n- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\n  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\n- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\n\nAll errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\nnormalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\nURL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\nfallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\ninactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\nwhen capture is inactive.\n\nThe existing consent surfaces remain:\n\n- `entire trust` grants trust for the current normalized repository identity;\n- `entire trust --revoke` withdraws trust previously granted for that\n  repository;\n- the interactive pre-push prompt offers \"Yes\", \"Not now\", and \"Always\"; and\n- users may edit the same trust fields directly in the settings file.\n\nAll writers use one identity derivation and one atomic settings update. A small\nmachine-local trust-grant ledger records which origin/path keys a CLI or\npre-push grant wrote for each canonical worktree. Re-granting after an identity\nchange removes superseded keys from that worktree's prior grant before writing\nthe new identity; revoke removes both the current identity and every key in that\nrecord. Hand-authored entries not recorded as a grant are never heuristically\ndeleted. This preserves origin-wide trust while fixing origin-to-path and\npath-to-origin stale grants. Status reports the exact currently matched trust\nsource.\n\nLocally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\nbehavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\ninferred from committed settings.\n\n### Push behavior\n\nPre-push preserves the current trust flow. If an untrusted globally activated\nrepository has no pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing and does not\nprompt. If checkpoints are pending and a real interactive terminal is\navailable, the hook opens the three-choice trust prompt using `/dev/tty`, never\nGit's stdin:\n\n- \"Yes\" records the current repository identity and drains the queued\n  checkpoints after re-evaluating policy;\n- \"Not now\" keeps capturing locally and leaves the queue for a later push; and\n- \"Always\" records `trust_all`, re-evaluates policy, and drains the queue.\n\nIf the prompt is unavailable, aborted, or fails—or if persistence or policy\nre-evaluation fails—the checkpoint queue remains held while the user's normal\nbranch push continues. Accessible mode must use a supported explicit selection\npath or hold; it must never accept a default implicitly, panic, or consume\npre-push stdin. The held message remains actionable and points to `entire trust`\nand the resolved settings file.\n\nEvery checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\nDoctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\ngate merely because it is interactive.\n\n## Agent hook model\n\nEach supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\n\n- installation;\n- recognition and version checks;\n- deduplication between user and repository scope;\n- removal of stale Entire entries;\n- expected counts; and\n- doctor/status completeness reporting.\n\nThe declarative specification preserves the complete existing event set and\ncommand behavior. In particular, Gemini continues installing all twelve current\nentries: `SessionStart`, both `SessionEnd` matchers, `BeforeAgent`, `AfterAgent`,\n`BeforeModel`, `AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`,\n`PreCompress`, and `Notification`. Events that currently parse to no lifecycle\nevent remain installed because their presence is part of the supported Gemini\nintegration contract. Claude Code likewise preserves its current lifecycle and\ntool-use matcher coverage. The refactor deduplicates how these entries are\ndeclared and reconciled; it does not prune or reinterpret them.\nLegacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\nreconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\ncommands.\n\nAn agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\nlazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\nsettings or re-running Git probes.\n\n### Hook reconciliation operations\n\nUser-scope reconciliation runs only from these integration-changing entry\npoints:\n\nconfiguration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\nonly. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\nrepo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\n\nEach agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\nread-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\nenable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\ndoctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\nprocess or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\n\nLazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\ncurrent Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\n`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\ndoctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\nmake a later hook repair it.\n\n## Status, doctor, and errors\n\nStatus reports:\n\n- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\n- the resolved settings-file path;\n- activation source for the current repository;\n- runtime layout;\n- trust source or held state; and\n- user-hook installation health.\n\nDoctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\nthe global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\nsettings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\n\nPolicy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\nmigration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\nuser's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\naborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\nbehavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\nother established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\nto fail that push.\n\n## Findings disposition\n\n| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove the takeover migration gate; keep the trust prompt outside route-selection locking | Non-pending pre-push is silent; pending interactive pre-push prompts through `/dev/tty`; concurrent hook and push do not block on route selection |\n| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\n| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\n| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\n| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Preserve the prompt but require an explicit supported selection; otherwise hold | `ACCESSIBLE=1` never grants the default implicitly, never consumes Git stdin, and never panics; explicit selections retain Yes/Not now/Always semantics |\n| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\n| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Preserve trust/revoke and record the exact keys written by each CLI/prompt grant so later re-grant/revoke can remove its stale identities without deleting hand-authored entries | Origin→path→origin, path→origin→path, and multiple fetch/push URL cases leave only the intended grant and revoke every recorded identity |\n| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\n\nTwo older GitHub comments are also covered: the preserved prompt derives its\nfolder-versus-origin label from the same identity used by the gate and writer,\nand the hold message remains concise and actionable.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\n### Policy matrix\n\nTable-driven tests cover combinations of:\n\n- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\n- local activation marker absent or present;\n- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\n- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\n- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\n- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\n\nEvery case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\ndecision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\n\n### Runtime and concurrency\n\n- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\n- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\n- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\n- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\n  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\n  recorded independently.\n- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\n- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\n  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\n- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\n  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\n- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\n  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\n  in both cases.\n- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\n- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\n  because those components no longer exist.\n\n### Hooks and security\n\n- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\n- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\n- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\n- Claude Code and all twelve current Gemini entries remain present with the same\n  command forms, matchers, deduplication, and event behavior.\n- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\n  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\n- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\n  malicious committed-value test.\n- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\n  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\n- Accessible mode cannot grant consent implicitly or consume pre-push stdin;\n  explicit Yes/Not now/Always choices retain their current effects.\n- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\n  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\n\n### Integration\n\nReal-binary integration tests cover:\n\n1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\n2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> interactive Yes/Not now/Always behavior,\n   plus non-interactive successful branch push with held checkpoints;\n3. `entire trust`, pre-push trust, and settings-file origin/path trust -> queued\n   checkpoint drain;\n4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\n   session visibility;\n5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\n   behavior; and\n6. linked-worktree isolation.\n\nRun focused tests during development and `mise run check` before every commit.\nRun deterministic canaries as part of the repository check. Real-agent E2E tests\nremain opt-in and require explicit user authorization.\n\n## Landing strategy\n\nThe refactor is prepared from `main` as one coherent review unit, not as another\nlayer on the old stack. The implementation should be organized into logical\ncommits:\n\n1. policy and provenance model;\n2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\n3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\n4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\n5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\n6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\n\nPR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\ncoherent final implementation rather than the current stacked merge history.\nBefore any remote update, lint and the full repository check must pass. After a\npush, all repository and PR gates must reach terminal status; risk/security\nmetrics are compared and every valid finding is addressed before completion.\n\nTrail findings are resolved only after the corresponding implementation and\nregression test are present on the trail branch.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\n\nThis is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\n"},"started_at_ms":1787608020127,"completed_at_ms":1787608020128}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T21:47:00.184Z","ordinal":9706,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a035bd-b4d8-78e0-99a2-7accad040fd0","call_id":"call_vY05i69M0UJJxSGloIAH9qEO","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"477f65\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002417,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5169,\"output\":\"# Settings-Only Global Tracking Refactor\\n\\n**Status:** Revised after product-behavior clarification; awaiting approval\\n**Date:** 2026-08-24\\n**Target:** consolidated global-tracking change, trail #1074 / PR #2030\\n**Supersedes:** `2026-08-24-global-takeover-migration-hardening-design.md` for the unmerged global-tracking feature\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nThe consolidated global-tracking change currently combines five stacked layers:\\n\\n1. user-global settings and hook gates;\\n2. invisible per-worktree runtime storage;\\n3. `enable --global`, an ask-once wizard, and runtime takeover migration;\\n4. user-level agent hooks plus status and doctor surfaces; and\\n5. per-repository checkpoint-egress trust.\\n\\nThe combined implementation is behaviorally integrated, but later layers adapted\\nthe APIs and storage decisions of earlier layers. The result contains multiple\\nactivation predicates, settings parsing duplicated in the path package, repeated\\nagent-hook specifications, and a runtime migration protocol that every active hook\\nmust participate in.\\n\\nThe desired product contract is now narrower:\\n\\n- global activation and exclusion policy are edited in the user's\\n  `~/.config/entire/settings.json` (or the path selected by\\n  `ENTIRE_CONFIG_DIR`);\\n- the CLI does not provide commands or wizard flows that turn global tracking\\n  on or off;\\n- the existing pre-push trust prompt, `entire trust`/`--revoke`, and\\n  settings-backed egress gate remain product behavior;\\n- supported user-level agent hooks are ordinary installed infrastructure and are\\n  inert until repository policy activates them, without changing Claude Code\\n  or Gemini CLI event coverage; and\\n- existing repository-level `entire enable` behavior remains supported.\\n\\nTrail #1074 currently has eight open findings: five high, two medium, and one\\nlow. This design treats them as acceptance criteria. It removes the unsafe state\\ntransitions rather than applying isolated fixes to the migration architecture.\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n- Make the user settings file the sole direct configuration surface for global\\n  activation and exclusions while preserving the existing trust UX and its\\n  settings-backed consent writes.\\n- Preserve a byte-clean worktree for repositories activated only by global\\n  policy.\\n- Install supported user-level hooks independently of global opt-in and make\\n  their inactive fast path safe and cheap.\\n- Evaluate repository activation, runtime layout, and egress consent once per\\n  process through one typed policy result.\\n- Never treat cloned repository content as evidence that this developer enabled\\n  or trusted the repository.\\n- Never execute repository-supplied commands in a repository activated only by\\n  global policy.\\n- Avoid moving live runtime data when repository policy changes.\\n- Preserve existing explicitly enabled repositories and linked-worktree\\n  isolation.\\n- Preserve the current pre-push trust choices, egress hold/drain behavior, and\\n  Claude Code/Gemini CLI lifecycle coverage.\\n- Resolve every current trail finding with a regression test or by removing the\\n  affected behavior.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n\\n- A CLI editor for global settings.\\n- A global-enablement wizard.\\n- Redesigning the pre-push trust experience, `entire trust`, checkpoint egress,\\n  or the supported Claude Code/Gemini CLI event surfaces.\\n- Supporting user-level hooks for agents without a verified user-scope config\\n  and deduplication contract.\\n- Changing session binding across repositories or implementing per-repository\\n  checkpoints for multi-repository turns.\\n- Moving every existing explicitly enabled repository to hidden git-side\\n  storage.\\n- Running real-agent E2E tests unless explicitly requested under repository\\n  policy.\\n\\n## User contract\\n\\n### Global activation is file-only\\n\\nThe `global` block remains part of the strict user settings schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"global\\\": {\\n    \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n    \\\"exclude_paths\\\": [],\\n    \\\"exclude_paths_exact\\\": [],\\n    \\\"exclude_origins\\\": [],\\n    \\\"trust_all\\\": false,\\n    \\\"trusted_origins\\\": [],\\n    \\\"trusted_paths\\\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe refactor removes the CLI mutation paths for global activation:\\n\\n- `entire enable --global`;\\n- `entire disable --global`;\\n- the ask-once global-tracking wizard question.\\n\\n`entire trust`, `entire trust --revoke`, and the interactive pre-push choices\\nremain. They may update only egress-consent fields (`trust_all`,\\n`trusted_origins`, and `trusted_paths`) in the same user settings file; they do\\nnot enable global tracking or change exclusions. Status and doctor continue to\\nreport both activation and trust state.\\n\\n### User-level hooks are infrastructure\\n\\nEntire installs and reconciles user-level hooks for agents with verified support\\n(currently Claude Code and Gemini CLI) during normal install/update integration\\nand the explicit integration-changing commands listed below. Doctor may repair\\nmissing or stale hook entries without changing global policy.\\n\\nThe installed hooks are inert unless either local activation or the user-global\\npolicy activates the current repository. A user who never enables global\\ntracking therefore retains only the cost of the minimal fail-closed gate.\\n\\nHook reconciliation never runs recursively inside an agent or Git hook. Adding\\na supported agent after Entire is installed is repaired by the next eligible\\nintegration-changing command or doctor repair.\\n| Re-enable after disable | Set local activation `enabled` and reuse the preserved route |\\n| `disable --uninstall` | Remove this worktree's runtime/route data, but retain a minimal `disabled` activation tombstone so always-installed user hooks cannot recreate the integration while global settings remain on. Shared clone Git hooks remain when global policy or another worktree still needs them |\\n| Enable after uninstall | Establish a fresh route from current policy and replace the tombstone with `enabled` |\\n| Legacy enabled, single worktree | Bootstrap only from enabled settings plus a Git-dir Entire hook or verified untracked worktree runtime evidence |\\n| Legacy enabled, linked worktree | Bootstrap only from verified untracked evidence in that exact worktree; otherwise report that `entire enable` must be run once there |\\n| Legacy disabled | Remain inactive; no activation bootstrap |\\n| Corrupt/unknown marker | Hooks skip Entire work; status/doctor report it; explicit `entire enable` may atomically replace it as the authorized recovery operation |\\n\\nIf repo settings persistence succeeds but route or activation persistence fails,\\n`entire enable` returns a clear incomplete-activation error. User hooks remain\\ninactive without the local marker unless independently activated by valid global\\npolicy; rerunning enable is the recovery path.\\n\\n## Egress trust\\n\\n### Settings-backed consent\\n\\nFor a repository activated only by global policy, checkpoint egress requires an\\nactive global tier and a current match in the user settings file:\\n\\n- `global.trust_all` is true;\\n- every configured fetch and push URL for the current origin normalizes and\\n  matches `global.trusted_origins`; or\\n- the canonical current worktree path matches `global.trusted_paths`.\\n\\nAll errors fail closed. If origin has multiple fetch or push URLs, every URL must\\nnormalize and every normalized key must be trusted; one unparseable or untrusted\\nURL holds egress. Path trust is considered only when origin is absent, not as a\\nfallback around a partially untrusted origin. `trust_all` does not make an\\ninactive global tier active and does not independently authorize an egress path\\nwhen capture is inactive.\\n\\nThe existing consent surfaces remain:\\n\\n- `entire trust` grants trust for the current normalized repository identity;\\n- `entire trust --revoke` withdraws trust previously granted for that\\n  repository;\\n- the interactive pre-push prompt offers \\\"Yes\\\", \\\"Not now\\\", and \\\"Always\\\"; and\\n- users may edit the same trust fields directly in the settings file.\\n\\nAll writers use one identity derivation and one atomic settings update. A small\\nmachine-local trust-grant ledger records which origin/path keys a CLI or\\npre-push grant wrote for each canonical worktree. Re-granting after an identity\\nchange removes superseded keys from that worktree's prior grant before writing\\nthe new identity; revoke removes both the current identity and every key in that\\nrecord. Hand-authored entries not recorded as a grant are never heuristically\\ndeleted. This preserves origin-wide trust while fixing origin-to-path and\\npath-to-origin stale grants. Status reports the exact currently matched trust\\nsource.\\n\\nLocally activated repositories retain their established repository-level sync\\nbehavior. Local activation is recorded by the machine-local marker rather than\\ninferred from committed settings.\\n\\n### Push behavior\\n\\nPre-push preserves the current trust flow. If an untrusted globally activated\\nrepository has no pending checkpoints, the hook emits nothing and does not\\nprompt. If checkpoints are pending and a real interactive terminal is\\navailable, the hook opens the three-choice trust prompt using `/dev/tty`, never\\nGit's stdin:\\n\\n- \\\"Yes\\\" records the current repository identity and drains the queued\\n  checkpoints after re-evaluating policy;\\n- \\\"Not now\\\" keeps capturing locally and leaves the queue for a later push; and\\n- \\\"Always\\\" records `trust_all`, re-evaluates policy, and drains the queue.\\n\\nIf the prompt is unavailable, aborted, or fails—or if persistence or policy\\nre-evaluation fails—the checkpoint queue remains held while the user's normal\\nbranch push continues. Accessible mode must use a supported explicit selection\\npath or hold; it must never accept a default implicitly, panic, or consume\\npre-push stdin. The held message remains actionable and points to `entire trust`\\nand the resolved settings file.\\n\\nEvery checkpoint-egress entry point consumes the same `RepoPolicy` decision.\\nDoctor or another explicitly invoked maintenance path does not bypass the trust\\ngate merely because it is interactive.\\n\\n## Agent hook model\\n\\nEach supported agent has one declarative hook specification that drives:\\n\\n- installation;\\n- recognition and version checks;\\n- deduplication between user and repository scope;\\n- removal of stale Entire entries;\\n- expected counts; and\\n- doctor/status completeness reporting.\\n\\nThe declarative specification preserves the complete existing event set and\\ncommand behavior. In particular, Gemini continues installing all twelve current\\nentries: `SessionStart`, both `SessionEnd` matchers, `BeforeAgent`, `AfterAgent`,\\n`BeforeModel`, `AfterModel`, `BeforeToolSelection`, `BeforeTool`, `AfterTool`,\\n`PreCompress`, and `Notification`. Events that currently parse to no lifecycle\\nevent remain installed because their presence is part of the supported Gemini\\nintegration contract. Claude Code likewise preserves its current lifecycle and\\ntool-use matcher coverage. The refactor deduplicates how these entries are\\ndeclared and reconciled; it does not prune or reinterpret them.\\nLegacy user/repository hook forms are normalized during foreground\\nreconciliation so correctness does not depend on byte-identical historical\\ncommands.\\n\\nAn agent-hook process resolves `RepoPolicy` once. Root pre-run, hook dispatch,\\nlazy setup, logging, and lifecycle code reuse that snapshot rather than reloading\\nsettings or re-running Git probes.\\n\\n### Hook reconciliation operations\\n\\nUser-scope reconciliation runs only from these integration-changing entry\\npoints:\\n\\nconfiguration already contains an Entire entry. It modifies user-scope config\\nonly. Repository-scope legacy normalization occurs only during an explicit\\nrepo-mutating `enable`, `configure`, or confirmed doctor repair.\\n\\nEach agent config uses a bounded per-file lock and an atomic, lossless\\nread-modify-write that preserves non-Entire entries. Installer and ordinary\\nenable paths report a warning and continue if user-hook reconciliation fails;\\ndoctor reports an unresolved repair. Recursion suppression prevents a hook\\nprocess or a child Entire invocation from entering reconciliation.\\n\\nLazy per-clone Git setup uses a versioned component record derived from the\\ncurrent Git-hook and checkpoint-ref specifications, replacing the stale\\n`GlobalSetupCompleted` boolean. A hook reruns only missing/outdated components;\\ndoctor reports the same component state and does not need to clear a latch to\\nmake a later hook repair it.\\n\\n## Status, doctor, and errors\\n\\nStatus reports:\\n\\n- whether global tracking is configured and enabled;\\n- the resolved settings-file path;\\n- activation source for the current repository;\\n- runtime layout;\\n- trust source or held state; and\\n- user-hook installation health.\\n\\nDoctor validates configuration and repairs hook infrastructure, but never writes\\nthe global settings block. It reports malformed patterns, unsafe/symlinked local\\nsettings, missing hooks, and route-marker corruption with actionable guidance.\\n\\nPolicy classification, route selection, activation-marker, and removed\\nmigration-gate failures are fail-closed for Entire work and fail-open for the\\nuser's Git or agent operation: log and skip capture or checkpoint sync without\\naborting the host operation. This rule does not weaken existing privacy-critical\\nbehavior in locally activated repositories. In particular, an OPF rewrite or\\nother established safety gate that currently fails a local pre-push continues\\nto fail that push.\\n\\n## Findings disposition\\n\\n| Finding | Design disposition | Required regression coverage |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `01M0TSFS8WSCD7CNSV5XJ4Q983` — migration gate blocks behind prompt/condensation | Remove the takeover migration gate; keep the trust prompt outside route-selection locking | Non-pending pre-push is silent; pending interactive pre-push prompts through `/dev/tty`; concurrent hook and push do not block on route selection |\\n| `01M0TSFQ9D1EH7M7VCZ43FYVC6` — gate failure aborts commit/push/session | Remove gate; policy/route failures skip Entire work | Separate forced-failure tests prove commit, push, and agent-session host operations succeed; local OPF safety failure still aborts push |\\n| `01M0TSEWA8GW6BMQEM98Y5AYMS` — committed project settings grant egress | Require machine-local activation or user-global trust; committed settings are not consent | Fresh clone with committed `enabled: true` neither gains local activation nor egress |\\n| `01M0TSE2YQG4NHX4HQPK7P7FW6` — symlinked `.entire` bypasses executable-setting trust | Reject symlink-traversing local settings and use an explicit hook-time field whitelist | Real clone fixture with tracked `.entire` symlink plus malicious OPF, checkpoint destination, external-agent, hook-path, and strategy values cannot influence hook execution or egress |\\n| `01M0TSDJQKTMR26TPV0Z1N7YG8` — accessible prompt auto-grants or panics | Preserve the prompt but require an explicit supported selection; otherwise hold | `ACCESSIBLE=1` never grants the default implicitly, never consumes Git stdin, and never panics; explicit selections retain Yes/Not now/Always semantics |\\n| `01M0TSGCWR0EVVQWK526A4BTEZ` — already-expired try-lock context | Remove try-lock and takeover migration | Barrier-controlled concurrent route-selection test proves both callers return the same complete winner and local enable records activation separately |\\n| `01M0TSEY7XNH32BR7ZXEGEMABS` — revoke misses stale origin after identity flip | Preserve trust/revoke and record the exact keys written by each CLI/prompt grant so later re-grant/revoke can remove its stale identities without deleting hand-authored entries | Origin→path→origin, path→origin→path, and multiple fetch/push URL cases leave only the intended grant and revoke every recorded identity |\\n| `01M0TSGEJB4B513AV3TS310RN0` — disable message contradicts `trust_all` | Remove global disable command; require active policy in egress decision | Disabled global tier is inactive even with `trust_all: true` |\\n\\nTwo older GitHub comments are also covered: the preserved prompt derives its\\nfolder-versus-origin label from the same identity used by the gate and writer,\\nand the hold message remains concise and actionable.\\n\\n## Testing strategy\\n\\n### Policy matrix\\n\\nTable-driven tests cover combinations of:\\n\\n- user-global absent, disabled, enabled, malformed, or excluded;\\n- local activation marker absent or present;\\n- project `enabled` absent, true, or false;\\n- settings path ordinary, tracked, symlinked, unreadable, or malformed;\\n- origin absent, normalized, changed, or unparseable; and\\n- trust absent, global, origin, or path based.\\n\\nEvery case asserts activation source, inactive reason, runtime layout, egress\\ndecision, and trust source from one policy evaluation.\\n\\n### Runtime and concurrency\\n\\n- Global-first route remains git-side after ordinary `entire enable`.\\n- Explicit-first route remains worktree-local after global settings change.\\n- Simultaneous first global hook and explicit enable converge on one route.\\n- A synchronization-barrier race between conflicting proposals publishes one\\n  complete route; both callers reread the same winner, and local activation is\\n  recorded independently.\\n- Linked worktrees receive distinct markers and runtime roots.\\n- Enabling a main worktree does not locally activate a newly added sibling that\\n  contains committed `enabled: true` settings.\\n- Legacy bootstrap accepts only the enumerated per-worktree record/file shapes;\\n  an external `core.hooksPath` is insufficient even in a single-worktree repo.\\n- Enable-after-uninstall selects the worktree route with global policy off and\\n  the git-common route with global policy on, replacing the disabled tombstone\\n  in both cases.\\n- Route-resolution failure skips capture without failing the host operation.\\n- No runtime migration, transcript rewrite, or long-held flock is exercised\\n  because those components no longer exist.\\n\\n### Hooks and security\\n\\n- User hooks are installed idempotently regardless of global opt-in.\\n- Disabled global policy reaches the cheapest fail-closed path.\\n- Agent hook specifications drive install, recognition, removal, and count.\\n- Claude Code and all twelve current Gemini entries remain present with the same\\n  command forms, matchers, deduplication, and event behavior.\\n- Committed and symlinked repository settings cannot activate executable\\n  behavior or egress in a fresh clone.\\n- Every executable/outbound field in the provenance matrix is pinned by a\\n  malicious committed-value test.\\n- Multiple fetch and push URLs all require explicit trust; an unparseable or\\n  untrusted secondary URL holds egress.\\n- Accessible mode cannot grant consent implicitly or consume pre-push stdin;\\n  explicit Yes/Not now/Always choices retain their current effects.\\n- `status`, `help`, `version`, `auth token`, and JSON inspection commands create\\n  no route, activation, settings, or agent-hook files.\\n\\n### Integration\\n\\nReal-binary integration tests cover:\\n\\n1. settings-file enable -> agent hook activity -> hidden capture;\\n2. untrusted pending checkpoint -> interactive Yes/Not now/Always behavior,\\n   plus non-interactive successful branch push with held checkpoints;\\n3. `entire trust`, pre-push trust, and settings-file origin/path trust -> queued\\n   checkpoint drain;\\n4. explicit enable after global capture -> same runtime route and continuous\\n   session visibility;\\n5. fresh clone with committed settings -> no local activation or executable\\n   behavior; and\\n6. linked-worktree isolation.\\n\\nRun focused tests during development and `mise run check` before every commit.\\nRun deterministic canaries as part of the repository check. Real-agent E2E tests\\nremain opt-in and require explicit user authorization.\\n\\n## Landing strategy\\n\\nThe refactor is prepared from `main` as one coherent review unit, not as another\\nlayer on the old stack. The implementation should be organized into logical\\ncommits:\\n\\n1. policy and provenance model;\\n2. sticky runtime-route marker and removal of takeover migration;\\n3. settings-only global surface and egress behavior;\\n4. declarative, automatically reconciled user hooks;\\n5. status/doctor/documentation updates; and\\n6. integration coverage and removal of obsolete tests/helpers.\\n\\nPR #2030 may retain its public review location, but its head should present the\\ncoherent final implementation rather than the current stacked merge history.\\nBefore any remote update, lint and the full repository check must pass. After a\\npush, all repository and PR gates must reach terminal status; risk/security\\nmetrics are compared and every valid finding is addressed before completion.\\n\\nTrail findings are resolved only after the corresponding implementation and\\nregression test are present on the trail branch.\\n\\n## Alternatives considered\\n\\n### Delete commands but retain takeover migration\\n\\nThis is the smallest textual change, but it preserves repeated policy reads,\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a035bb-e411-7b32-a380-13e06970de42","create_time":1787608020.184897}}}
