{"timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:13:04.476Z","ordinal":10720,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a0360c-81dc-7a11-9990-4fb84c7bba7e","call_id":"call_B26mgk3wBqYoJOzzI9vsQ3fg","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/efficiency_audit\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Clarity pass complete. The strongest result: `--global` is still fully present, while the merged design retains duplicated settings policy, mixed command scope, and substantial migration/test scaffolding. I sent the prioritized findings and from-scratch recommendations to the parent agent.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/task1_policy_inputs\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/task1_quality_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"### Strengths\\n\\n- The leaf has the intended dependency direction and does not import `paths`, parent `settings`, `session`, `strategy`, or `logging`.\\n- Disabled, unconfigured, and invalid exclusion configurations return before repository resolution.\\n- The lock spans the complete load–mutate–atomic-write transaction, and cache invalidation follows successful persistence.\\n- Parent APIs use explicit functions/type aliases, while the established worktree identity/hash implementation is shared through a cycle-safe leaf.\\n- Focused package tests, the `repopolicy` race test, and Windows cross-compilation passed.\\n\\n### Issues\\n\\n#### Critical\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n#### Important\\n\\n- `cmd/entire/cli/settings/repopolicy/user_settings.go:62`: `LoadUserSettings` performs only one `Decode`, so a valid enabled configuration followed by malformed trailing bytes is accepted. This violates the strict-load/fail-closed requirement and can activate global tracking from a malformed file. After decoding `UserSettings`, decode a second `json.RawMessage` and require `io.EOF`; add cases for trailing junk and a second JSON value.\\n\\n- `cmd/entire/cli/settings/repopolicy/repository.go:151`: the normalization error embeds the raw origin URL, and `settings/global.go:99` forwards that error to logging unchanged. An unnormalizable URL such as one containing user-info credentials can therefore expose secrets; relying on the usual pre-logger gate to suppress the debug record is unsafe for this exported leaf and future callers. Use `gitremote.RedactURLOrPath(origin)` or omit the origin value, and add a test asserting credentials never occur in the returned error.\\n\\n#### Minor\\n\\n- `cmd/entire/cli/settings/repopolicy/types.go:60`: moving `GlobalConfig` into its canonical owner discarded the detailed field contracts covering glob versus exact matching, symlink behavior, multi-URL trust, and fail-closed semantics. This makes later route/trust work easier to implement incorrectly. Move the existing per-field documentation to the leaf without changing the JSON schema.\\n\\n### Recommendations\\n\\n- Add `ResolveRepositoryAt` integration cases for nested working directories, symlinked roots, and main/linked worktrees using `--separate-git-dir`; existing `paths` tests validate the shared identity helper but not the complete new resolver.\\n- Add a golden marshal test covering every `GlobalConfig` JSON key and `UserSettings.Global == nil`.\\n- Consider a subprocess concurrency test so the lock contract is verified across processes, not only goroutines.\\n\\n### Assessment\\n\\nReady to proceed? With fixes. The architecture and transaction design are sound, but strict decoding currently accepts malformed trailing content and one new error path can expose raw remote credentials.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a035c5-5c5e-7ca3-86ab-631a16cc0d4a","create_time":1787613184.476478}}}
